om the dmem path, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the divider and raising median Fmax to 77.218 MHz.\n15:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r5s2 (regression, +11.18%): Registering the alternate fetch PC alongside the branch-miss bit preserves one-bubble recovery on a ready bus through the fetch queue's empty bypass and shifts the Gowin critical path to the EX ALU feeding dmem, where one-hot operand forwarding raised median Fmax from 60.380 to 64.667 MHz and cleared the 60.84 MHz hurdle for the 3,979,945-cycle stalled CoreMark run.\n16:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r6s0 (regression, -29.31%): In the two-di\n…[2538 of 5610 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:45e0c473e3ff1b1f]…\nrds into the flow-through issue FIFO during imem stalls saved 5.64% of stalled CoreMark cycles, with same-cycle EX recovery and issue control paths to ID/EX valid and the FIFO pointer resets limiting the retimed design to 80.894 MHz median Gowin Fmax against an 85.238 MHz promotion target.\n25:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r9s0 (regression, +3.27%): Registering one-hot EX result selects and sharing SRL/SRA in a signed 33-bit shifter cut the measured shift path from 11.505 to 7.706 ns in Gowin placement 0, while the divider quotient-to-remainder path in the other two placements capped median Fmax at 89.183 MHz, below the 90.33 MHz needed for promotion at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n26:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r9s1 (regression, +0.52%): Reusing fall-through packets from a shared decoded store saved 6.60% of stalled CoreMark cycles, and registering the issue head still left the EX redirect path to the request slot register capping median Gowin Fmax at 81.083 MHz, below the 84.38 MHz needed for promotion.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
