{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\nls -la cores/bench\nrg -n 'RAW_READ|operand_sel|REGISTERED_SELECT|implementation_notes' cores/bench .gitignore\"", "aggregated_output": "\"\"\"End-to-end pipeline tests for rtl/core.sv.\n\nDrives a minimal Python-side imem/dmem against the core's combinational\nfetch/load model and captures RVFI retirements. Covers the §8 phase-2\nacceptance set: forwarding (EX->EX, MEM->EX), branches, JAL, JALR,\nSW+LW roundtrip, load-use stall, and the trap discipline (illegal /\nECALL trap, EBREAK does NOT trap), plus the RV32M result interlock.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.clock import Clock\nfrom cocotb.triggers import FallingEdge, RisingEdge, Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import run_cocotb\n\n\n# ── RV32I instruction encoder helpers ──────────────────────────────────────\ndef _r(funct7, rs2, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((funct7 & 0x7F) << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _i(imm, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((imm & 0xFFF) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _s(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    imm_hi = (imm >> 5) & 0x7F\n    imm_lo = imm & 0x1F\n    return (imm_hi << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (imm_lo << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _b(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    # imm is a 13-bit signed number; bit 0 is implicitly zero.\n    b12  = (imm >> 12) & 1\n    b105 = (imm >> 5)  & 0x3F\n    b41  = (imm >> 1)  & 0xF\n    b11  = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    return (b12 << 31) | (b105 << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (b41 << 8) | (b11 << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _j(imm, rd, opcode):\n    # 21-bit signed imm; bit 0 implicit zero.\n    b20    = (imm >> 20) & 1\n    b101   = (imm >> 1)  & 0x3FF\n    b11    = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    b1912  = (imm >> 12) & 0xFF\n    return (b20 << 31) | (b101 << 21) | (b11 << 20) | (b1912 << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef ADDI(rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0010011)\ndef ADD (rd, rs1,\n…[396661 of 399733 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a3e7e8aab325aea5]…\nal recovery select logic. Its Gowin trial measured 82.802, 84.340, and 84.340 MHz with 4,829 logic cells. An alternate direct-target adder and recovery select reached 81.700 MHz median. The retained median yields 231.01 CoreMark iterations/sec, below the 250.19 baseline, with forwarding into the EX ALU limiting the median placements.\\n- Local `formal/run_all.sh` did not finish within the implementation budget. It generated 53 checks. The completed PC backward, uniqueness, liveness, causality, and cover checks passed. No counterexample had been reported at handoff.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T18:14:03.212256Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r13s2 (regression, -7.67%): Registering loop bounds in parallel with in-place issue FIFO replay admission shifted the Gowin timing bottleneck from EX recovery feedback into the loop-tail register to operand forwarding into the EX ALU, where the drop to 84.340 MHz median Fmax outweighed the 5.61% reduction in stalled CoreMark cycles.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
