{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_105","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check\ncat implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Forward ADDI from the registered address sum\n\nIssue registers two select bits per operand. The first gives an eligible EX/MEM writer priority over the older pair. The second selects the address or ALU result in the EX/MEM pair, or MEM/WB or the register-file snapshot in the older pair. Both producer comparisons exclude writes to x0, so x0 reads the register file's zero.\n\nID decodes the immediate and register forms of ADD separately. A fast ADDI forwards the registered full-width effective address and takes that same value through its one-hot MEM writeback lane. A true late dependency takes the existing MEM adder when the predecessor writes. When it traps, ADDI uses the ordinary address sum from the older operand. A match on encoded rs2 alone updates the RVFI read value while the immediate still supplies the addend.\n\nRegister ADD receives forwarded rs2 directly. The other operations use the existing ALU operand selection. The ALU result merge groups the arithmetic terms and EX groups the link and AUIPC terms. Memory requests still launch from the registered address, with the existing stall and recovery controls.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint and all 115 cocotb cases passed. The added coverage includes both forwarding ports, signed immediate wraparound, mixed ALU and ADDI producer priority, EX and MEM squash, store backpressure, and late ADDI completion with a writing or trapping predecessor.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed for selftest and three random programs, each on both ready and stalled buses, plus canonical stalled CoreMark. CoreMark validated in 3,868,036 marker cycles with no out-of-bounds access.\n- A five-cycle bounded SAT check of the issue, EX and MEM datapath passed for symbolic operand values, displacements, tags, producer choices, late matches, issue bubbles, and holds. It compared the captured address and writeback sum to full-width arithmetic and checked both reported operand values.\n- The stock `formal/run_all.sh` selected the single-retire wrapper and ALTOPS configuration. The run is in progress.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n`python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options` ran the vendor flow for all three placements.\n\n| Placement option | Candidate Fmax (MHz) | Champion Fmax (MHz) | Candidate limiting path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 86.645 | 87.120 | EX/MEM ALU result through forwarding and the ALU back to EX/MEM |\n| 1 | 91.354 | 96.774 | EX/MEM ALU result through forwarding and the ALU back to EX/MEM |\n| 2 | 91.354 | 96.774 | EX/MEM ALU result through forwarding and the ALU back to EX/MEM |\n\nThe 91.354 MHz median yields 236.18 CoreMark iterations/s, down 5.60% from 250.19 and below the 101.23 MHz promotion threshold. The candidate uses 4,692 logic cells, 1,148 registers, four block RAMs and three DSPs. In the median placements, divider feedback and fetch recovery also land within 0.02 ns of the limiting ALU feedback path. The separate EX and older member-select variant measured an 84.841 MHz median with the original ALU result merge and an 83.192 MHz median with the grouped result merge.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
