{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_81","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 MPLCONFIGDIR=\"$TMPDIR/bench-matplotlib\" python3 - <<'\"'PY'\nfrom tools.orchestrator import allowed_patterns_for, path_is_allowed\nfrom tools.sandbox import dirty_state\npatterns = allowed_patterns_for('bench')\nfor path in sorted(dirty_state()):\n    print(path)\n    assert path_is_allowed(path, patterns), path\nPY\nsed -n '1,120p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/multiplier.sv\ncores/bench/test/test_alu.py\ncores/bench/test/test_multiplier.py\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\nimplementation_notes.md\n# Registered RV32M multiplier\n\nThe bench core now executes MUL, MULH, MULHU and MULHSU in a shared two-cycle multiplier. Launch registers four signed 17-by-17 limb products and the requested result half. Two carry-save compression levels and one final adder form product bits 63:16. Gowin inferred four MULT18X18 primitives and one ALU54D.\n\nEX launches from forwarded operands after older data-memory backpressure clears. Launch holds fetch and ID/EX, inserts an EX/MEM bubble, and captures the operands in the same RVFI registers used by DIV/REM. EX/MEM accepts the result and instruction metadata together. Immediate consumers use the existing EX/MEM bypass. The multiplier retains the four exact ALTOPS formulas and uses the same handshake for both arithmetic implementations.\n\nThe ALU lane in the one-hot result mux is qualified by the decoded instruction class. The multiply lane uses registered completion. Branch and jump recovery is qualified separately by control-flow decode, so the combined multiply and divide wait feeds the pipeline interlock without adding a dependency to redirect. The divider keeps its existing launch, six work cycles, and transfer.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint passed with real arithmetic and with ALTOPS.\n- All 79 cocotb cases passed. The multiplier suite checks 16,969 directed and seeded arithmetic vectors plus result latency, backpressure, and reset. The pipeline test checks a stalled store, consecutive dependent multiplies, and an immediate store consuming the second result for both address and data.\n- Co-simulation passed selftest and all three random programs against the ISS in zero-wait mode and with both stall streams. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC gate with both stall streams.\n- Local formal passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL, including liveness and register consistency. The unchanged `formal/run_all.sh` ran from a temporary copy.\n\n## Measured fitness\n\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax | Worst reported path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 52.803 MHz | 24.233 ns, divider |\n| 1 | 53.987 MHz | 23.817 ns, divider |\n| 2 | 53.987 MHz | 23.817 ns, divider |\n\nThe median is **53.987 MHz**. The 2K, `-O3`, 10-iteration CoreMark run used **4,291,450 marker-window cycles**, 2.21% more than the champion. The resulting fitness is **125.80 iterations/s**, 7.70% above 116.81 iterations/s. `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1` reproduced these measurements in the temporary copy. The longest reported setup paths are in the divider, and each multiply adds one EX cycle.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
