nly fetch and decode bubbles removes the fetch NOP mux and ID/EX payload clear from redirect feedback, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the EX-to-dmem path and raising median Fmax from 45.134 to 47.560 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (regression, +8.99%): With valid-only squashes, registering bypass tag matches in ID and qualifying them with producer write eligibility at the one-hot EX operand mux removes tag comparisons and binary select encoding from execute, yielding a 49.189 MHz Gowin median at 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles with the multiply-result path to dmem setting the timing limit.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (improvement, +16.24%): Fetch-time JAL prediction and 64-entry gshare cut CoreMark cycles by 6.52% by avoiding taken-transfer bubbles, while carrying IF's direct targets into EX reduced recovery to a direction check and a one-hot EX result mux raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.967 to 49.042 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 (regression, +7.70%): Registering the shared RV32M multiplier's four 17-by-17 partial products before carry-save accumulation shifted the Gowin timing limit from the EX ALU to the divider, and the median Fmax gain from 49.042 to 53.987 MHz outweighed the 2.21% increase in CoreMark marker cycles from one extra EX cycle per multiply.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +10.97%): Completing RV32M in MEM removes M results from the EX mux feeding dmem addresses and gives the divider an extra work edge at EX/MEM handoff, while the load-use interlock adds a single bubble for dependent consumers before MEM/WB forwarding.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r3s2 (regression, +6.03%): A two-entry flow-through fetch queue cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 5.10% by filling during decode holds and draining during imem stalls, while the EX forwarding and ALU path to data RAM limited median Fmax to 49.349 MHz and left throughput below the round's MEM-stage RV32M winner.\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
