{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_64","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short; nl -ba implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/divider.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_divider.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n     1\t# Divider launch lookup\n     2\t\n     3\tThe divider now decodes the divisor magnitude's low nibble into 16 one-hot rows. Each row has eight 16-bit truth tables indexed by the dividend magnitude's high nibble. The row output packs the prefix quotient above the prefix remainder. The zero row supplies quotient 15 and remainder A. The existing raw-operand range check selects the lookup for magnitudes below 16 and supplies quotient zero and remainder A for larger divisors.\n     4\t\n     5\tBoth launch magnitudes use five parallel slices with boundaries at bits 0, 8, 16, 24, 28, and 32. A negative operand complements each slice and supplies its increment directly from the lower raw bits. The lookup uses a dedicated four-bit divisor magnitude and ORs the masked rows in groups of four for Gowin mapping. The launch edge loads the prefix state and the full-width divisor multiples. The state machine still executes seven work edges with two full-width radix-4 steps apiece. The final-edge MEM handoff, held completion, EX operand capture for RVFI, sign correction, and ALTOPS substitutions are preserved.\n     6\t\n     7\tThe divider tests cover signed operands on both sides of each interior slice boundary and at the boundary itself. They exercise the new cases as dividends and divisors, along with the existing exhaustive nibble, divide-by-zero, signed overflow, exact-latency, and held-result coverage.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Local verification\n    10\t\n    11\t- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. All seven cocotb suites passed.\n    12\t- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final implementation. No formal fixes or retries were needed.\n    13\t- A SAT miter against the champion proved equality of every divider register after launch for all operands and operation codes. A one-step induction also proved cycle-by-cycle equality of divider state and outputs.\n    14\t- Co-simulation passed for selftest and all three random ELFs in both stall modes, and for CoreMark with instruction and data stalls enabled.\n    15\t- Canonical 2K CoreMark at `-O3` passed the UART validation and all five CRC checks. Ten iterations took 4,233,123 marker cycles, matching the champion.\n    16\t- Gowin placements 0, 1, and 2 measured 55.989, 59.974, and 59.974 MHz. The 59.974 MHz median gives 141.68 iterations/s, 9.30% above the 129.62 iterations/s champion. The median placement is limited by the forwarding and EX redirect path to the fetch PC. Placement 0 falls below the 57.394 MHz acceptance target. The scored median clears it by 2.580 MHz.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
