{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_133","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,125p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Registered fetch recovery\n\nID/EX captures the alternate direct successor with the applied prediction. A predicted-taken transfer saves PC+4, and a predicted-not-taken transfer saves the direct target. IF computes the fallthrough word once and supplies it to ID. EX still resolves the architectural next PC and applies alignment and link-write checks. The advancing miss pulse drives the existing same-edge ID/EX squash and qualifies capture of either the saved direct successor or the forwarded JALR target.\n\nThe registered miss pulse selects the full-width recovery word for the next imem request, PC-relative target calculation, and gshare index. A ready response advances to its predicted successor. A stalled response seeds the normal fetch PC so the request stays in place when the pulse clears. Reset clears the pulse and normal PC.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All 96 cocotb cases in seven runners passed, including gshare direction reversals, forwarded JALR, misaligned targets, and the load-use and divide interlocks. The extended recovery test holds a branch behind an older store and checks a retirement gap of two cycles with a ready recovery response and four cycles with two response stalls. It also checks that the recovered JAL issues its predicted successor as soon as the response arrives.\n- Co-simulation passed eight byte-exact RVFI trace comparisons across selftest and three random programs, each with ready buses and with instruction and data stalls. The canonical stalled CoreMark run passed all CRCs and the UART validation, with 4,233,123 marker cycles and zero out-of-bounds accesses.\n- `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the first run of the selected RTL. `reg_ch0` completed after 24 minutes of solver wall time.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\nPlacement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 83.541, 87.297, and 87.297 MHz on both runs. The 87.297 MHz median is 1.973 MHz below the promotion threshold and gives 206.22 iterations/s, a 2.29% gain over the champion's 201.60 iterations/s. Placement 0 is limited by the branch comparator path to the recovery pulse. Placements 1 and 2 are limited by the EX-to-data-memory path. Alternate branch comparison, miss qualification, JALR adder sharing, and fetch retry implementations yielded lower placement medians.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
