{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_196","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'SAT proof finished|ERROR:' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/bench-direct-alu-proof.log\" \"$TMPDIR/bench-direct-idex-proof.log\" \"$TMPDIR/bench-direct-ex-result-proof.log\"; cat implementation_notes.md; git diff --check'","aggregated_output":"/srv/hwebench/tmp/7c172beb/0f7d6bb8/bench-direct-alu-proof.log:480:SAT proof finished - no model found: SUCCESS!\n/srv/hwebench/tmp/7c172beb/0f7d6bb8/bench-direct-ex-result-proof.log:1081:SAT proof finished - no model found: SUCCESS!\n# Predecode EX result selection and share the right shifter\n\nID predecodes the integer result lanes and captures their enables and the arithmetic fill bit with the ID/EX payload. Payload capture follows `issue_candidate`, including on the redirect edge where `issue` clears validity. Both right shifts use one signed 33-bit shift. The extra high bit is `arithmetic_fill & a[31]`, and the result lane takes the low 32 bits. A separate left shifter feeds the same masked-OR mux as the right shifter. The encoded `alu` wrapper uses the shared datapath.\n\nJAL and JALR select PC+4 as their sole result lane. The EX alignment check still qualifies the link for forwarding and writeback. Address generation, branch comparison, RV32M and RVFI source capture continue to consume the ordinary forwarded operands.\n\nThe pipeline tests cover immediate and register SLL, SRL and SRA on a negative operand at counts 0, 1, 16 and 31. They check forwarded register counts with populated upper bits, adjacent retirement, a held SRA followed by a queued SRL, JAL/JALR link consumers, and alignment traps that preserve the destination register.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint and all seven bench cocotb runners passed.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and all three random programs against the ISS with both ready and stalled memory. Stalled CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 3,979,945 marker cycles with the canonical CRCs (`e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a`, `fcaf`) and `Correct operation validated.`\n- A combinational SAT equivalence check passed for the encoded `alu` against the champion over all opcodes and operands.\n- The local single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` run has passed 11 of 53 checks so far, including cover, liveness, illegal instruction, unique order, and both PC checks. The suite is incomplete.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n| Placement option | Fmax (MHz) | Whole-core limiting period (ns) | Right shift from operand bit 9 to result bit 5 (ns) | Critical path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 82.781 | 12.080 | 7.706 | MEM/WB result through EX recovery to ID/EX validity |\n| 1 | 89.183 | 11.213 | 8.373 | Divider quotient to divider remainder |\n| 2 | 89.183 | 11.213 | 8.373 | Divider quotient to divider remainder |\n\nThe 89.183 MHz median yields 224.08 CoreMark iterations/s, below the 90.33 MHz promotion threshold at the measured cycle count. The worst reported EX result paths were 12.051 ns in option 0 and 11.212 ns in options 1 and 2, through arithmetic carry logic.\n\nThe right-shift column is a register-to-register estimate from the longest path over Gowin's maximum-corner SDF arcs, including clock-to-Q, setup and clock skew. The SDF builds reproduced the Fmax values from the standard timing runs. In option 0, the same method measured 11.505 ns for the champion's bit-9 to bit-5 path. The champion's STA report puts that path at 11.327 ns before setup and 11.362 ns including setup. The longest estimated path from any ID/EX `rs1` bit to a lower, nonzero EX/MEM result bit was 10.467 ns in option 0 and 10.304 ns in options 1 and 2.\n\nPlacement 0 used 4,329 logic cells and 966 registers. The champion used 4,224 logic cells and 954 registers. The direct masked-OR mux had the highest median among the tested implementations. The alternatives explored earlier shift operand selection, different mux groupings, and a shared add/sub lane.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
