{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_22", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'split|ALU|divider|timing|103|101.23' cores/bench/LESSONS.md; ls -l cores/bench/experiments\"", "aggregated_output": "1:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +619.88%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM on the shared EX path with registered radix-4 rounds raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 39.190 MHz, with four cascaded steps per round setting the new timing limit and CoreMark's divide-free marker window staying at 4,491,485 cycles.\n2:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +729.13%): Reducing the shared divider from four cascaded radix-4 steps per cycle to three moved the critical path out of the divider and raised median Gowin Fmax from 39.190 to 45.134 MHz, with a nine-cycle retirement gap after a redirect or load-use bubble on ready buses still within the ten-cycle liveness bound.\n3:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +3.96%): A fetch-local 16-entry tagged BTB saved 5.73% of CoreMark cycles by avoiding redirect bubbles on correct predictions, with the EX ALU still on the critical path and a drop in median post-placement Fmax from 5.442 to 5.334 MHz limiting the throughput gain.\n4:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.37%): Using valid-only fetch and decode bubbles removes the fetch NOP mux and ID/EX payload clear from redirect feedback, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the EX-to-dmem path and raising median Fmax from 45.134 to 47.560 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n5:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (regression, +8.99%): With valid-only squashes, registering bypass tag matches in ID and qualifying them with producer write eligibility at the one-hot EX operand mux removes tag comparisons and binary select encoding from execute, yielding a 49.189 MHz Gowin median at 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles with the multiply-result path to dmem setting the timing limit.\n7:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 (regression, +7.70%): Registering the shared RV32M multiplier's four 17-by-17 partial products before carry-save accumulation shifted the Gowin timing limit from the EX ALU to the divider, and the median Fmax gain from 49.042 to 53.987 MHz outweighed the 2.21% increase in CoreMark marker cyc\n…[4780 of 7852 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:19bd4bb16c92432d]…\n to 87.114 MHz with the registered EX ALU result critical in all three placements and CoreMark cycles unchanged.\n25:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r9s0 (regression, +3.27%): Registering one-hot EX result selects and sharing SRL/SRA in a signed 33-bit shifter cut the measured shift path from 11.505 to 7.706 ns in Gowin placement 0, while the divider quotient-to-remainder path in the other two placements capped median Fmax at 89.183 MHz, below the 90.33 MHz needed for promotion at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n28:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r10s1 (improvement, +15.30%): A parallel MEM lane captures the ready operand in EX and uses the registered MEM/WB result to let load-to-branch and multiply-to-add pairs issue back-to-back, saving 2.81% of stalled CoreMark cycles, while shortening the divider and EX-to-data-address paths helped raise median Gowin Fmax from 84.107 to 96.774 MHz.\ntotal 356\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup 285014 Nov 22 14:05 log.jsonl\n-rw-rw----+ 1 nobody nogroup  77799 Nov 22 14:05 progress.png\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
