)\n\n\ndef _j(imm, rd, opcode):\n    # 21-bit signed imm; bit 0 implicit zero.\n    b20    = (imm >> 20) & 1\n    b101   = (imm >> 1)  & 0x3FF\n    b11    = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    b1912  = (imm >> 12) & 0xFF\n    return (b20 << 31) | (b101 << 21) | (b11 << 20) | (b1912 << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef ADDI(rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, \n…[20981 of 24053 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c03f7c5807e740cf]…\n01-r12s0 (regression, -1.85%): Registering the low 28 dividend bits’ nonzero flag and the one-hot divisor row at launch moved divider initialization out of Gowin’s 25 worst setup paths, while ID-to-EX load/store address generation in two placements capped median Fmax at 94.983 MHz versus 96.774 MHz at the same CoreMark marker cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r12s1 (regression, -3.79%): Deferring dependent AND, OR, and XOR to MEM after splitting the barrel shifter at EX/MEM kept stalled CoreMark cycles within 0.09% of the champion, while EX forwarding into load/store address generation capped median Gowin Fmax at 93.185 MHz versus 96.774 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r12s2 (regression, -2.49%): Separate issue FIFO payload banks for live fetch and replay preserved 3,549,156 stalled CoreMark cycles and shifted the Gowin timing limit from the tail write-data mux to EX load/store address generation, with MEM/WB forwarding capping median Fmax at 86.585 MHz, below the 92.881 MHz needed for promotion.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
