{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_115","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short && sed -n '1,45p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Load and store address generation\n\nID registers the existing one-hot rs1 forwarding decision for decoded loads and stores, plus a displacement-only select for x0. EX computes the three candidate low sums in parallel with the ordinary operand mux, selects the corrected byte address bits [12:0], and checks alignment. EX/MEM also captures the selected raw upper base, the carry-or-borrow condition, and one propagation bit for each upper address bit. MEM applies the upper XOR correction and uses the reconstructed address for both the data bus and aligned RVFI. The EX/MEM hold covers every address component during a data-bus stall.\n\nThe selected implementation adds dedicated copies of bits [12:0] of the EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding results. Gowin preserves these flops to reduce the load that the low address adders place on the shared forwarding nets. The copies use the same reset and hold conditions as their source results. The displacement sign is included in each 13-bit sum, so the selected bit 12 XOR raw bit 12 gives the same adjustment as the selected low carry XOR displacement sign. The version wired directly to the shared forwarding registers measured 86.886 MHz with the same address arithmetic.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint, 116 cocotb cases, and `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed. ISS cosim covered selftest and all three random ELFs on ready and stalled buses. The pipeline tests check forwarding priority, x0, carry and borrow, full-width wrap, MMIO, byte lanes, held requests, load-use timing, alignment traps, and wrong-path store suppression.\n- A focused, bounded Yosys SAT check compared the EX and MEM address lane with full-width addition of a sign-extended displacement. It passed for all base selections, base values, displacements, alignments, and symbolic stalls and recovery, including bus qualification and the aligned RVFI address and masks.\n- Canonical stalled CoreMark validated all CRCs and completed ten iterations in 3,868,036 marker cycles. There were no out-of-bounds accesses.\n- The stock single-channel ALTOPS formal run on the final RTL hit the 14-minute local timeout. It generated 53 checks and passed five by the cutoff: retirement cover, liveness, uniqueness, causality, and backward PC. The other 48 checks remain unverified. The logs show solver waits and no counterexample, so no formal repair retries were made.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n| Placement option | Fmax (MHz) | Block RAMs | Worst setup path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 82.137 | 4 | ID/EX rs2 forwarding select through the ALU to EX/MEM result |\n| 1 | 90.676 | 4 | EX/MEM result through forwarding and the ALU back to EX/MEM result |\n| 2 | 90.676 | 4 | EX/MEM result through forwarding and the ALU back to EX/MEM result |\n\nThe 90.676 MHz median yields 234.42 CoreMark iterations/s, below the 101.23 MHz promotion threshold at this cycle count. Gowin reports 4,807 logic cells and 1,198 registers, an increase of 46 cells and 50 registers over the champion. The address-sum and upper-correction endpoints fall outside the 25 worst setup paths in each report. Option 0 also has MEM recovery into ID/EX within 0.003 ns of its limiting ALU path. Options 1 and 2 show divider remainder feedback and recovery within 0.031 ns of the limiting setup slack.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
