{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_148","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git status --short && git diff --check && sed -n '1,100p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# Capture fast producer values on the consumer’s issue edge\n\nID/EX captures each encoded source from the youngest eligible EX writer or the write-first register file. A MEM match registers a MEM/WB select and leaves the snapshot zero. The separate ALU B snapshot inserts the immediate during issue. Encoded rs2 retains its full value for stores, branches, RV32M and RVFI.\n\nThe issue muxes apply tag-qualified masks directly to the unmasked ADD and SUB results. A shared lane carries the other ALU results, jump links and AUIPC. EX/MEM merges the same lanes for writeback. Late loads, RV32M and deferred ADD retain their existing completion paths. Registered recovery suppresses younger writes and memory requests, and the divider retains the launch operands for RVFI.\n\nThe implementation also registers fast-writer eligibility and FIFO emptiness. The FIFO bit uses the existing push, pop and recovery decisions. A low two-bit JALR sum supplies the alignment check, which qualifies the shared lane and the decision to fall back to an older source. Gowin synthesis attributes preserve the arithmetic, shared and register-file inputs to the capture muxes.\n\nVerilator lint, all seven cocotb runners and stock cosim passed. Directed tests cover the three fast lanes, an immediate with a matching encoded rs2, a stalled store, a trapping JALR that would otherwise supply a link, and recovery after capturing a speculative store operand. Canonical stalled CoreMark reported `e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a` and `fcaf` and printed “Correct operation validated.” It completed in 3,868,036 marker cycles, matching the champion. With ready buses, both versions took 3,064,374 marker cycles. Selftest and three random traces matched the champion for 34,301 retirements in each mode, including cycle, encoded source data and active memory data. Two selftest DIV retirements had different inactive RVFI write data with zero write masks.\n\n| Gowin placement | Fmax (MHz) | Worst setup path | ALU to ID/EX (ns) | Address (ns) | Recovery (ns) | Divider (ns) |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0 | 86.090 | FIFO read pointer to ALU B capture | 11.614 | 11.577 | 11.584 | 11.603 |\n| 1 | 88.933 | Registered MEM recovery to ALU B capture | 11.233 | Outside worst 25 | 11.244 | 11.236 |\n| 2 | 88.933 | Registered MEM recovery to ALU B capture | 11.233 | Outside worst 25 | 11.244 | 11.236 |\n\nThe 88.933 MHz median implies 229.92 stalled CoreMark iterations/s at the measured cycle count. This is below the champion’s 96.774 MHz median and the 101.23 MHz promotion threshold. The original fused RTL measured 77.814 MHz. Explicit mux boundaries, registered eligibility and FIFO emptiness raised the median. Enabling snapshots during all cycles free of EX holds measured 82.576 MHz, so the delivered RTL uses issue-qualified capture.\n\nThe initial formal invocation stopped at Yosys parsing `$bits` with a typedef argument. Replacing that expression with a typed mux fixed elaboration. The unchanged single-channel liveness check passed on the timing refinement. The full formal suite is still running and has reported zero counterexamples.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
