{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_170","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,54p' implementation_notes.md; git diff --numstat\"","aggregated_output":"# Reciprocal divider implementation\n\nThe bench divider now uses the nine-edge reciprocal schedule for DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU. The existing phase counter still exposes readiness before the ninth edge, allows acceptance on that edge, and holds the final state when acceptance is deferred. The existing EX recovery reset cancels a squashed operation. ALTOPS captures its substitute result on the same final edge.\n\n- The 512×34 synchronous ROM initializes from constant intercept and slope formulas. Interpolation registers the upper bits of `R*M - ((I << 22) + 1)` and complements them to recover the exact downward-rounded seed. This ordering lets Gowin infer a DSP multiply-subtract.\n- The Newton and quotient stages retain full product widths before slicing. The quotient shift always discards bit 0 of the scaled quotient, so the masked product uses bits [32:1] and slices at bit 31. The registered mask lets that product start in parallel with the quotient shift.\n- Edge nine computes the residual and precomputes the signed base result and corrected quotient. MEM uses a 33-bit residual subtraction for the correction decision and computes the remainder sign prefix in parallel. The result selection covers zero divisors and the 32-bit signed overflow case.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed in normal and ALTOPS builds. All eight bench pytest runners passed, including fixed-latency signed and unsigned division, all 512 ROM intervals, normalization boundaries, exact multiples, deferred acceptance, reset at every divider phase, and dependent instructions after recovery. The ALTOPS runner checked the ninth-edge handoff for all four operations.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` passed the RVFI comparison with and without stalls and validated the stalled CoreMark CRCs. The explicit NRET matches the bench core's single-channel configuration. CoreMark completed ten iterations in 3,868,036 marker cycles.\n- The stock `formal/run_all.sh` suite was launched with the bench single-channel configuration. The stock `liveness_ch0` and separately launched `insn_divu_ch0` checks passed. The aggregate suite was still running at the last check, with no counterexample reported.\n- A bounded Yosys SAT comparison against the prior ALTOPS divider matched its handshake over 15 edges and its result with symbolic operands for immediate and deferred acceptance.\n\n## Gowin\n\n| Placement option | Fmax |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 89.307 MHz |\n| 1 | 88.462 MHz |\n| 2 | 88.462 MHz |\n| Median | 88.462 MHz |\n\nThe inferred design uses one pROMX9 for the coefficients, 12 DSP blocks in total, and 4,736 logic cells. The worst setup paths cross fetch recovery in option 0 and EX operand forwarding in options 1 and 2. The 88.462 MHz median falls below the 101.23 MHz promotion threshold and yields 228.70 iter/s.\n\nA grouped-prefix variant used 4,288 logic cells and reached 88.071 MHz median. I kept the faster variant because CoreMark iterations per second determine promotion.\n171\t226\tcores/bench/rtl/divider.sv\n80\t13\tcores/bench/test/test_divider.py\n31\t0\tcores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
