{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_90","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'verify_dir=\"$TMPDIR/bench-fetch.ccg9Xk8v\"\nrg '\"'DONE \\\\((PASS|FAIL|ERROR)' \\\"\"'$verify_dir/formal/last_run-compare.log\" | tail -n 25\ndate -u +%T\nsed -n '\"'1,220p' implementation_notes.md\nverilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv\ngit diff --check\"","aggregated_output":"SBY  8:20:14 [unique_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY  8:20:21 [pc_bwd_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY  8:21:06 [causal_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY  8:21:18 [liveness_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY  8:26:12 [cover] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\n07:34:55\n# Register fetch address candidates before steering\n\nFetch captures the predicted target, sequential successor, replay address, and EX recovery address in parallel. The one-hot steering registers give EX recovery priority on the same edge that flushes the FIFO and squashes issue. The predicted lane holds 18 word-address bits. Fetch qualifies it with the full computed target and alignment, and the issue packet carries the full architectural target to EX. The sequential, replay, and recovery lanes each hold 30 word-address bits.\n\nEX shares the low 31-bit comparison between signed and unsigned branches. The operand sign bits resolve comparisons with differing signs, and the low bit of funct3 inverts complementary conditions. This change reduces logic on the branch resolution path feeding recovery. The fetch-only build reached 78.081 MHz median, with a redirect path limiting one placement and divider paths limiting the other two.\n\nThe directed pipeline tests cover a ready JAL blocked by a full FIFO, a redirect coincident with a younger taken prediction, replay of a stalled recovery target, and sequential release of the last-word hold. They check fetch addresses, retirement timing and order, and data-bus side effects. They also exercise signed and unsigned branch ordering for mixed-sign, same-sign, and equal operands.\n\nVerilator lint and all seven bench cocotb runners passed. Selftest and the three random programs matched the ISS with ready and stalled memory. Canonical stalled CoreMark passed the CRC and completion checks and took 3,979,945 marker cycles for 10 iterations.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 83.351, 87.114, and 87.114 MHz. The 87.114 MHz median yields 218.88 iterations/s, below the 90.33 MHz promotion threshold at this marker count. The longest path in all three placements ends at the registered EX ALU result. For options 1 and 2, the redirect and divider paths are within 0.002 ns of the ALU path.\n\nYosys equivalence checks against the champion passed for the fetch and execute stages. An additional bounded SAT check passed for fetch starting from reset. The single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` suite is still running in a scratch copy with the stock wrapper and check configuration.\n- V e r i l a t i o n   R e p o r t: Verilator 5.047 devel rev v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6 (mod)\n- Verilator: Built from 0.800 MB sources in 16 modules, into 0.403 MB in 4 C++ files needing 0.000 MB\n- Verilator: Walltime 0.151 s (elab=0.036, cvt=0.086, bld=0.000); cpu 0.074 s on 1 threads; allocated 24.910 MB\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
