{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_77","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"for f in cores/bench/generated/timing/gowin_p*/gw.log; do printf '%s ' \\\"\"'$f\"; tail -3 \"$f\"; done; cat implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/generated/timing/gowin_p0/gw.log [60%] Routing Phase 0 completed\n[70%] Routing Phase 1 completed\n[80%] Routing Phase 2 completed\ncores/bench/generated/timing/gowin_p1/gw.log [10%] Placement Phase 0 completed\n[20%] Placement Phase 1 completed\n[30%] Placement Phase 2 completed\ncores/bench/generated/timing/gowin_p2/gw.log [10%] Placement Phase 0 completed\n[20%] Placement Phase 1 completed\n[30%] Placement Phase 2 completed\n# Registered loop replay\n\nFetch captures taken conditional branch observations from EX or MEM, with MEM supplying the older observation when both stages resolve on the same cycle. The observation is checked for aligned endpoints in the 1 MiB aperture and a backward span of up to 64 words, including both endpoints. The interval governs fills. Resident entries keep their exact tags when a different interval is trained.\n\nThe two parity banks register their current and successor matches from the selected registered request. A hold or sequential advance can use a staged match during an imem stall. Taken predictions and recoveries discard the preceding matches and start a lookup at the new request. Replay stores raw instructions, a narrow target, and branch/JAL classification bits. It uses the current gshare lookup and enqueues at the tail of the four-entry logical FIFO. Ready fetch retains its empty-queue bypass. Eight physical slots permit a tentative tail write each cycle.\n\nLive and replay targets have independent registered request candidates. The live packet carries the low target, alignment bit, carry, and displacement sign. EX reconstructs the wrapped upper target for recovery and RVFI. FENCE.I invalidates the banks and staged fills at acceptance. A pending-fence count gates replay and loop training and fills, while the ordinary gshare predictor continues to train from resolved branches. Recovery clears squashed fences and accounts for a fence accepted at the corrected PC.\n\nVerilator lint and the full cocotb suite passed. Co-simulation used `NRET=1` to match the core manifest and covered four ELFs both ready and stalled. The CoreMark CRCs passed with 3,549,156 stalled marker cycles. Yosys rejected `$bits` applied to the replay entry type during an intermediate formal run. The declaration now takes `$bits` of the packed payload signal, and the full single-issue suite is running on that revision. Gowin is measuring all three placements with the FIFO and replay banks mapped to distributed RAM.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
