{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short && sed -n '1,170p' implementation_notes.md && python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; from xml.etree import ElementTree as E; cases=[c for p in Path(\\\"cores/bench/test\\\").glob(\\\"*.result.xml\\\") for c in E.parse(p).findall(\\\".//testcase\\\")]; failures=[c.get(\\\"name\\\") for c in cases if c.find(\\\"failure\\\") is not None]; print(f\\\"Cocotb: {len(cases)} cases, {len(failures)} failures\\\"); raise SystemExit(bool(failures))'\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? implementation_notes.md\n# 16-entry tagged branch target buffer\n\n## Implementation\n\nIF indexes the BTB with `PC[5:2]`. An aligned fetch in the 1 MiB aperture uses the cached word target when the entry is valid, the `PC[19:6]` tag matches, and the direction counter is at least 2. The target array has one fetch read port. The tags and counters also have a training read port. Reset clears only the valid bits.\n\nEX trains on advancement. Eligible conditional branches store their direct target for either outcome and saturate their direction counter, starting at the corresponding weak state on replacement. Eligible JAL and JALR write a strongly taken entry with the resolved target. Training requires a legal control instruction and aligned source and target addresses within the instruction aperture.\n\nThe registered fetch PC holds the selected successor of the instruction in EX. EX compares the full resolved next PC against that register on every valid advance and uses the same address for RVFI. A mismatch squashes the younger instruction and overrides imem backpressure. The hazard unit generates the EX hold independently of fetch hazards so dmem backpressure defers both recovery and training. Misalignment traps resolve to the existing sequential successor.\n\nThe top-level source includes the shared type declarations conditionally so the requested glob-order lint command works alongside the package-first build flows.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with both the requested glob and `make lint TARGET=bench`.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-issue checks with zero failures using the original runner, wrapper, and check configuration staged under `$TMPDIR`.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all 83 cocotb cases. Directed pipeline tests check direction reversals and saturation, one training update through a dmem hold, conflicting tags, changing JALR targets, recovery with both bus stalls, and stale predictions on a non-control instruction and a misaligned jump.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench` passed all five ELFs. Selftest and the three random traces passed with both ready modes. CoreMark passed the canonical CRC and validation checks under imem and dmem stalls.\n\n## Tournament build measurements\n\n| Metric | Recorded baseline | BTB |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| CoreMark bracketed cycles for 10 iterations | 4,491,485 | 4,234,052 |\n| Gowin median Fmax | 5.442 MHz | 5.334 MHz |\n| CoreMark iterations per second | 12.12 | 12.60 |\n\nGowin reported 4.908, 5.334, and 5.334 MHz for placement options 0, 1, and 2. Option 0 used 12,665 logic cells, 509 registers, and 21 LUT-RAM cells. The longest setup path still crosses the EX ALU, and the lower median Fmax offsets part of the 5.73% cycle reduction. The measured throughput gain is 3.97%.\nCocotb: 83 cases, 0 failures\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
