ction's validity\\nneeds to change on a squash.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Wire raw imem data directly to decode. Capture the ID/EX payload on\\nevery edge outside a dmem or divider hold, including bubble cycles.\\nUpdate its valid bit separately. Reset clears valid, a dmem or divider\\nhold retains it, and on other cycles it becomes fetched-valid AND\\nNOT load-use. D\n…[28488 of 31560 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ae055866a16d5b1a]…\nl 78 cocotb cases passed. Directed tests cover consecutive counter aliasing, reversals in both directions, stalled recovery, misaligned and reserved direct transfers, forwarded JALR, and the exact nine-cycle cold BEQ and eight-cycle predicted JAL divider retirement gaps.\\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed all five ELFs. The trace ELFs passed with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark produced the required CRCs.\\n- Gowin measured 45.723, 49.042, and 49.042 MHz. The median of 49.042 MHz and 4,198,541 bracketed CoreMark cycles yielded 116.81 iterations/s, up 16.24% from 100.49 iterations/s.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T03:21:47.189680Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (improvement, +16.24%): Fetch-time JAL prediction and 64-entry gshare cut CoreMark cycles by 6.52% by avoiding taken-transfer bubbles, while carrying IF's direct targets into EX reduced recovery to a direction check and a one-hot EX result mux raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.967 to 49.042 MHz.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
