env[\"NRET\"] = str(read_nret(worktree_path / \"cores\" / target / \"core.yaml\"))\n    return env\n\n# Stall modes for the full-trace comparison. The stalled mode uses the same\n# bus backpressure as the fitness run, so logic that only acts while a\n# memory port stalls (which V2 now times and scores) is trace-checked too.\nTRACE_STALL_MODES = ((), (\"--istall\", \"--dstall\"))\n\n\ndef run_one(elf: Path, sim\n…[19532 of 22604 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1f4d249310fbbbc3]…\n enabled. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical CRC and completion checks.\\n- Gowin timing across placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 48.531, 49.349, and 49.349 MHz. `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1` reproduced the 49.349 MHz median and measured 3,984,611 bracketed CoreMark cycles. The score was 123.85 iterations/s, 6.03% above the champion's 116.81 iterations/s and above the 4.6% hurdle.\\n\\nThe limiting setup paths run from the register file through EX forwarding and the ALU to data RAM. The median has about 0.66 MHz of margin over the qualification frequency at the measured cycle count.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T03:50:15.511949Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r3s2 (regression, +6.03%): A two-entry flow-through fetch queue cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 5.10% by filling during decode holds and draining during imem stalls, while the EX forwarding and ALU path to data RAM limited median Fmax to 49.349 MHz and left throughput below the round's MEM-stage RV32M winner.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
