{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,240p' formal/run_all.sh; sed -n '1,245p' Makefile\"", "aggregated_output": "#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Run riscv-formal checks against the current $RTL_DIR/*.sv.\n# Requires: formal/riscv-formal cloned (manual clone or `git submodule init`).\n#\n# Stages every $RTL_DIR/*.sv plus wrapper.sv + the chosen checks config under\n# the riscv-formal tree, then runs sby -> bitwuzla via the framework's\n# generated makefile. Tallies PASS/FAIL by inspecting each task's\n# logfile.txt.\n#\n# Usage: bash formal/run_all.sh [checks-cfg-path]\n#   default: formal/checks.cfg       (fast, ALTOPS, used by orchestrator)\n#   deep   : formal/checks-deep.cfg  (no ALTOPS, proves M-ext arithmetic)\n#\n# Env vars (orchestrator-driven, override $1 and defaults):\n#   WRAPPER     — path to wrapper SV (default formal/wrapper.sv)\n#   CHECKS_CFG  — path to checks config (default formal/checks.cfg)\n# When BOTH are unset, the script auto-detects nret from\n# cores/$CORE_NAME/core.yaml: nret=1 routes to wrapper_si.sv +\n# checks_si.cfg; nret=2 (or absent) uses the defaults. Agents\n# invoking `bash formal/run_all.sh` directly therefore get the\n# right wrapper without needing to know the env-var plumbing.\nset -e\n\nif [ -z \"${RTL_DIR:-}\" ] || [ -z \"${CORE_NAME:-}\" ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: formal/run_all.sh requires RTL_DIR and CORE_NAME env vars.\" >&2\n  echo \"  Example: RTL_DIR=cores/v1/rtl CORE_NAME=v1 bash formal/run_all.sh formal/checks.cfg\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" && pwd)\"\nPROJECT_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\" && pwd)\"\nRISCV_FORMAL=\"$SCRIPT_DIR/riscv-formal\"\n# Per-invocation work dir + log, unique among every process sharing this\n# riscv-formal checkout (the K concurrent agents of a run share it through\n# their worktrees' symlink). The name used to be \"$CORE_NAME-$$\", but an\n# agent's sandboxed shell runs in its own PID namespace where $$ is 2 or 3\n# for every agent, so siblings wrote into one dir and wiped each other's\n# checks mid-SBY (V2 incident 03). mktemp gives a unique name instead.\n# FORMAL_WORK_ID pins the suffix so tools/eval/formal.py can remove exactly\n# its own dir afterwards. genchecks.py derives @core\n…[14886 of 17958 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:31e376d920a5a9eb]…\nree add \"$(WT_PATH)\" $(WT_BRANCH); \\\n\t  else \\\n\t    git worktree add -b $(WT_BRANCH) \"$(WT_PATH)\"; \\\n\t  fi; \\\n\t  [ -e \"$(WT_PATH)/formal/riscv-formal\" ] || ln -s \"$(CURDIR)/formal/riscv-formal\" \"$(WT_PATH)/formal/riscv-formal\"; \\\n\tfi\n\t@$(MAKE) -C \"$(WT_PATH)\" loop \\\n\t  WORKTREE= \\\n\t  TARGET=$(TARGET) N=$(N) K=$(K) AGENT=$(AGENT) \\\n\t  $(if $(strip $(BASE)),BASE=$(BASE),) \\\n\t  $(if $(strip $(COREMARK)),COREMARK=$(COREMARK),) \\\n\t  $(if $(strip $(LUT)),LUT=$(LUT),)\nelse\n\tAGENT_PROVIDER=$(AGENT) python3 -m tools.orchestrator $(ORCH_FLAGS) $(ORCH_TARGET_FLAG)\nendif\n\nreport:\n\tpython3 -m tools.orchestrator --report $(ORCH_TARGET_FLAG)\n\nclean:\n\trm -rf test/cosim/sim_build sim_build out\n\trm -rf experiments/worktrees\n\trm -f bench/programs/*.elf\n\trm -f test/*.result.xml\n\tfind test -name __pycache__ -prune -exec rm -rf {} + 2>/dev/null || true\n\tfind tools -name __pycache__ -prune -exec rm -rf {} + 2>/dev/null || true\nifneq ($(strip $(TARGET)),)\n\trm -rf $(OBJ_DIR)\n\trm -rf $(GEN_DIR)\n\trm -rf cores/$(TARGET)/sim_build\nendif\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git branch -a --list '*r4s2*'; git log --all --oneline --grep='queue' -20; git check-ignore -v cores/bench/implementation_notes.md implementation_notes.md; cat cores/bench/core.yaml; cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": ".gitignore:87:cores/*/implementation_notes.md\tcores/bench/implementation_notes.md\nname: bench\ndescription: Frozen LLM-benchmark fixture core. Identical RTL to baseline at the bench-fixture-v1\n  freeze point. The benchmark matrix clones from the bench-fixture-v1 orphan branch\n  and operates only on this directory.\nisa: rv32im\nnret: 1\ntarget_fpga: tang-nano-20k\ntargets:\n  fmax_mhz: 135\n  lut4: 10000\n  coremark_iter_s: 300\n  coremark_per_mhz: 2.2\ncurrent:\n  fmax_mhz: 61.864\n  lut4: 3376\n  ff: 801\n  coremark_iter_s: 2.0e-06\n  coremark_per_mhz: 0.0\n  source_id: hyp-20261122-002-r4s1\n  updated: '2026-11-22T04:15:19.443726Z'\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +619.88%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM on the shared EX path with registered radix-4 rounds raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 39.190 MHz, with four cascaded steps per round setting the new timing limit and CoreMark's divide-free marker window staying at 4,491,485 cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +729.13%): Reducing the shared divider from four cascaded radix-4 steps per cycle to three moved the critical path out of the divider and raised median Gowin Fmax from 39.190 to 45.134 MHz, with a nine-cycle retirement gap after a redirect or load-use bubble on ready buses still within the ten-cycle liveness bound.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +3.96%): A fetch-local 16-entry tagged BTB saved 5.73% of CoreMark cycles by avoiding redirect bubbles on correct predictions, with the EX ALU still on the critical path and a drop in median post-placement Fmax from 5.442 to 5.334 MHz limiting the throughput gain.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.37%): Using valid-only fetch and decode bubbles removes the fetch NOP mux and ID/EX payload clear from redirect feedback, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the EX-to-dmem path and raising median Fmax from 45.134 to 47.560 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (regression, +8.99%): With valid-only squashes, registering bypass tag ma\n…[1767 of 4839 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9f9db086518bfd38]…\nl divisors and computing operand magnitudes in parallel slices shifted the Gowin timing limit to the EX forwarding and redirect path to the fetch PC, raising median Fmax from 54.869 to 59.974 MHz at the same 4,233,123 CoreMark marker cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r4s1 (improvement, +12.74%): Registering forwarded DIV/REM operands before sign conversion and radix-4 prefix trials cut the Gowin critical path and improved throughput in CoreMark's divide-free marker window, with the extra divide cycle using the remaining slack in the ten-cycle ready-bus retirement bound after a redirect or late-result bubble.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 (regression, +12.31%): A four-entry LUT-RAM fetch queue cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 5.98% by fetching during decode holds, with registered occupancy isolating its write enable from EX recovery and the remaining recovery paths to the fetch PC and ID/EX valid bit limiting median Gowin Fmax to 57.942 MHz, leaving throughput 0.56 iterations/s behind the round winner.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
