{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path; data=json.loads(Path(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").read_text().splitlines()[-1]); print(data[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print({k: v for k,v in data.items() if k in [\\\"result\\\",\\\"fpga\\\",\\\"timing\\\",\\\"agent\\\"]})'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path; data=json.loads(Path(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").read_text().splitlines()[-1]); print(data[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print({k: v for k,v in data.items() if k in [\\\"result\\\",\\\"fpga\\\",\\\"timing\\\",\\\"agent\\\"]})'\"","aggregated_output":"Give the existing gshare and direct JAL predictor an independently\nadvancing request PC and a four-entry, flow-through circular queue of\ncomplete fetch packets. Use a plain packed-vector array sized with\n$bits(fetch_packet), a synchronous write at the tail and an asynchronous\nread at the head so Gowin infers distributed SRAM. Use two pointers\nwith an extra wrap bit. Every edge with a free tail writes the raw\nfetch packet into that unused slot. Only a ready response that is\nenqueued advances the write pointer. The physical write enable depends\nonly on registered occupancy. Reset and EX recovery clear the pointers.\nKeep a two-input select between the indexed head and a direct bypass\nof the raw packet when empty.\n\nAccept a ready response whenever the queue has space. An empty queue\nwith decode ready delivers the response by bypass without advancing\nits pointers. A queue with a free slot can push and pop together. A\nfull queue may pop and resumes accepting on the next cycle. Advance\nthe request PC on an accepted response and let decode drain queued\npackets during imem stalls. Each packet carries the full PC and direct\ntarget, instruction, applied direction, and exact gshare lookup index.\nTest IF/ID validity in the late-result interlock and use validity to\nrepresent an unready bypass. A load-use bubble leaves its packet pending.\nDivide and dmem holds block decode consumption while fetch fills space.\n\nAt 0x000ffffc, accept only by empty-queue bypass. If the prediction\nwould fall through past the aperture, hold the request on that word\nwith no valid bypass until its EX advance. Then follow the resolved\nsuccessor. EX recovery takes priority over all queue movement, fetch\nholds and imem readiness. Squash ID/EX by clearing its valid bit on\nreset or flush and clock the payload using only the existing decode\nhold. This keeps EX recovery off the wide queue write and ID/EX payload\npaths. Keep EX resolution and RVFI on the actual successor. Train on\neach advancing conditional branch using the saved lookup index.\n\nA scratch prototype on the current champion completed canonical stalled\nCoreMark in 3,979,945 bracketed cycles with all CRCs passing, saving 5.98%.\nGowin measured 55.996, 59.663 and 59.663 MHz across placement options,\nwith the worst path still in divider launch. The median yields 149.91\niterations/sec, 15.65% above 129.62. The design needs a median above\n53.961 MHz at that cycle count to clear the acceptance threshold. The\nprototype passed 84 cocotb cases, eight full RVFI trace comparisons\nacross both bus modes, and all 53 single-issue ALTOPS formal checks.\nRecheck timing and the existing correctness gates on the implementation.\n\n{}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"agent_message","text":"Cosim passed in both bus modes, and CoreMark reproduced the expected 3,979,945 marker cycles. Formal found that Yosys was treating the FIFO’s struct array as a single register. I’ll switch the storage to a plain packed-vector array and rerun formal and timing."}}
