{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_22", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'r5s2|r5s1|r6s2' cores/bench/LESSONS.md cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-1700\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Preselect all execute operands and isolate data address generation\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 146.14 CoreMark iterations/s at 61.864 MHz\\nover 4,233,123 marker cycles. Its worst Gowin path starts at the\\ninferred register-file block RAM and reaches data memory through\\nthe register-file write-first mux, EX bypass, the integer ALU and\\nthe jump-link mux. Prior decode-stage tag matching left the write-first\\ndata mux in series with EX bypass. A load or store needs only the\\nselected rs1 plus a signed 12-bit displacement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 146.14 CoreMark iterations/s at 61.864 MHz\\nover 4,233,123 marker cycles. Its worst Gowin path starts at the\\ninferred register-file block RAM and reaches data memory through\\nthe register-file write-first mux, EX bypass, the integer ALU and\\nthe jump-link mux. Prior decode-stage tag matching left the write-first\\ndata mux in series with EX bypass. A load or store needs only the\\nselected rs1 plus a signed 12-bit displacement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Partition the issue-to-memory path at the existing pipeline edges.\\nCompare both encoded sources in decode against the destinations\\ncurrently in EX and MEM. These producers will occupy the EX/MEM\\nand MEM/WB slots when the incoming instruction enters EX. Qualify\\neach with the actual next-stage write eligibility. Also compare the\\nsources with the current WB destination when it is writing. Snapshot\\nthat WB value in a shared 32-bit register on the same ID/EX capture\\nedge. Register four one-hot bits for each source in ID/EX. In priority\\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Register the predictor's alternate PC in parallel with branch resolution\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The four-entry gshare fetch queue in r4s2 saved 253,178 stalled CoreMark\\ncycles. Its longest Gowin setup path ran from the register file through EX\\nrec\n…[1201 of 4273 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f412291419052320]…\no-bit history, direct JAL prediction, and saved-index training.\\n\\nFor the instruction in EX, compute the sole possible misprediction\\ndestination using\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:14:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r5s1 (improvement, +24.82%): Capturing one-hot forwarding selects and a shared WB snapshot in ID/EX folds register-file write-first handling and EX bypass into one mux per operand, and a dedicated load/store address adder removes the ALU and jump-link mux from the dmem path, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the divider and raising median Fmax to 77.218 MHz.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:15:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r5s2 (regression, +11.18%): Registering the alternate fetch PC alongside the branch-miss bit preserves one-bubble recovery on a ready bus through the fetch queue's empty bypass and shifts the Gowin critical path to the EX ALU feeding dmem, where one-hot operand forwarding raised median Fmax from 60.380 to 64.667 MHz and cleared the 60.84 MHz hurdle for the 3,979,945-cycle stalled CoreMark run.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
