n and 64-entry gshare cut CoreMark cycles by 6.52% by avoiding taken-transfer bubbles, while carrying IF's direct targets into EX reduced recovery to a direction check and a one-hot EX result mux raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.967 to 49.042 MHz.\n- 2026-1\n…[2313 of 5385 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ffe3190a1b04df1f]…\ns the next bottleneck, so registering the matches alongside operands in ID/EX moved the Gowin median timing limit to the divider and raised Fmax from 61.864 to 75.404 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r5s1 (improvement, +24.82%): Capturing one-hot forwarding selects and a shared WB snapshot in ID/EX folds register-file write-first handling and EX bypass into one mux per operand, and a dedicated load/store address adder removes the ALU and jump-link mux from the dmem path, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the divider and raising median Fmax to 77.218 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r5s2 (regression, +11.18%): Registering the alternate fetch PC alongside the branch-miss bit preserves one-bubble recovery on a ready bus through the fetch queue's empty bypass and shifts the Gowin critical path to the EX ALU feeding dmem, where one-hot operand forwarding raised median Fmax from 60.380 to 64.667 MHz and cleared the 60.84 MHz hurdle for the 3,979,945-cycle stalled CoreMark run.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
