{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'ls -ad .agents .codex cores/AGENTS.md cores/bench/AGENTS.md AGENTS.md\nwc -l cores/bench/LESSONS.md cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\ncat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": ".agents\n.codex\nls: cannot access 'cores/AGENTS.md': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'cores/bench/AGENTS.md': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'AGENTS.md': No such file or directory\n    34 cores/bench/LESSONS.md\n    36 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\n    70 total\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +619.88%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM on the shared EX path with registered radix-4 rounds raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 39.190 MHz, with four cascaded steps per round setting the new timing limit and CoreMark's divide-free marker window staying at 4,491,485 cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +729.13%): Reducing the shared divider from four cascaded radix-4 steps per cycle to three moved the critical path out of the divider and raised median Gowin Fmax from 39.190 to 45.134 MHz, with a nine-cycle retirement gap after a redirect or load-use bubble on ready buses still within the ten-cycle liveness bound.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +3.96%): A fetch-local 16-entry tagged BTB saved 5.73% of CoreMark cycles by avoiding redirect bubbles on correct predictions, with the EX ALU still on the critical path and a drop in median post-placement Fmax from 5.442 to 5.334 MHz limiting the throughput gain.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.37%): Using valid-only fetch and decode bubbles removes the fetch NOP mux and ID/EX payload clear from redirect feedback, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the EX-to-dmem path and raising median Fmax from 45.134 to 47.560 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (regression, +8.99%): With valid-only squashes, registering bypass tag matches in ID and qualifying them with producer write eligibility at the one-hot EX operand mux removes tag comparisons and binary select encoding from execute, yielding a 49.189 MHz Gowin median at 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles with the multiply-result path to dmem setting the timing limit.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (improvement, +1\n…[9439 of 12511 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f3074495da0a9f1a]…\n (regression, -1.98%): Separate registered request targets for live fetch and branch replay leave a shared issue FIFO tail write-data mux, where the path from registered fetch selects limited all three Gowin placements and dropped median Fmax from 96.774 to 87.041 MHz, outweighing the 8.24% reduction in stalled CoreMark marker cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r12s0 (regression, -1.85%): Registering the low 28 dividend bits’ nonzero flag and the one-hot divisor row at launch moved divider initialization out of Gowin’s 25 worst setup paths, while ID-to-EX load/store address generation in two placements capped median Fmax at 94.983 MHz versus 96.774 MHz at the same CoreMark marker cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r12s1 (regression, -3.79%): Deferring dependent AND, OR, and XOR to MEM after splitting the barrel shifter at EX/MEM kept stalled CoreMark cycles within 0.09% of the champion, while EX forwarding into load/store address generation capped median Gowin Fmax at 93.185 MHz versus 96.774 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
