{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'hyp-20261122-001-r9s0|hyp-20261122-001-r8s0|hyp-20261122-002-r6s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Split divider rounds by divisor magnitude\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 182.41 CoreMark iterations/s at a 77.218 MHz Gowin\\nmedian over 4,233,123 marker cycles. Its critical path in all three\\nplacements crosses two dependent full-width radix-4 divider steps.\\nEarlier traces found no DIV/REM inside the marker window, but every\\ninstruction pays for the divider's clock period. A scratch redesign\\nshortened the divider path and exposed a register-file block RAM path\\nthrough EX to the fetch PC and an EX ALU path. The execute datapath\\nneeds a shorter divide recurrence and fabric-registered operands to\\nconvert that improvement into a faster core clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion scores 182.41 CoreMark iterations/s at a 77.218 MHz Gowin\\nmedian over 4,233,123 marker cycles. Its critical path in all three\\nplacements crosses two dependent full-width radix-4 divider steps.\\nEarlier traces found no DIV/REM inside the marker window, but every\\ninstruction pays for the divider's clock period. A scratch redesign\\nshortened the divider path and exposed a register-file block RAM path\\nthrough EX to the fetch PC and an EX ALU path. The execute datapath\\nneeds a shorter divide recurrence and fabric-registered operands to\\nconvert that improvement into a faster core clock.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the restoring divider by unsigned divisor magnitude D. Capture\\nthe forwarded operands at launch and keep the nine-edge handoff. For\\n0 < D < 2048, consume the leading dividend nibble at initialization.\\nFor D below 16 use a combinational table with one decoded row per\\ndivisor and 16-bit truth tables indexed by the dividend nibble. For\\nD of 16 or more, the nibble is already the remainder and its quotient\\nbits are zero. Run seven pairs of radix-4 steps on the remaining 28\\nbits. Since R < D throughout, each step uses an 11-bit remainder,\\n13-bit trial operands, and one extra bit for borrow. Use the low\\n13 bits of the wide path's regis\n…[19557 of 22629 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:def00d601a1e38a3]…\n STA report puts that path at 11.327 ns before setup and 11.362 ns including setup. The longest estimated path from any ID/EX `rs1` bit to a lower, nonzero EX/MEM result bit was 10.467 ns in option 0 and 10.304 ns in options 1 and 2.\\n\\nPlacement 0 used 4,329 logic cells and 966 registers. The champion used 4,224 logic cells and 954 registers. The direct masked-OR mux had the highest median among the tested implementations. The alternatives explored earlier shift operand selection, different mux groupings, and a shared add/sub lane.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T10:05:32.942592Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r9s0 (regression, +3.27%): Registering one-hot EX result selects and sharing SRL/SRA in a signed 33-bit shifter cut the measured shift path from 11.505 to 7.706 ns in Gowin placement 0, while the divider quotient-to-remainder path in the other two placements capped median Fmax at 89.183 MHz, below the 90.33 MHz needed for promotion at the same CoreMark cycle count.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
