{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261122-(002-r10s1|001-r12s0|002-r12s1)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; [print(d[\\\"id\\\"],d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for l in sys.stdin for d in [json.loads(l)]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r10s1\n# MEM completion lane\n\nFetch predecodes eligibility for BEQ, BNE, ADD, and ADDI and carries the flag with the issue packet. Packets at the last instruction-aperture word always use the ordinary EX route. Issue keeps the existing interlock for other consumers and captures both regular forwarding selectors and any pending encoded-source matches for eligible consumers. Deferred additions can supply the lane on the next cycle.\n\nEX captures the available operand and its ordinary fallback result, along with both forwarded operands for RVFI. EX checks memory alignment and qualifies the producer's write before its consumer enters MEM. MEM uses the registered MEM/WB result for equality or addition and handles a double match directly. ADDI retains its immediate when only its encoded rs2 matches, and RVFI still observes the producer's value for that tag. The EX ALU uses registered operation selects and one right shifter for SRL and SRA. Branch comparisons combine parallel byte comparisons.\n\nFetch registers the sequential, prediction, retry, and two recovery addresses. Direct targets use an 18-bit low-word sum and a 12-bit upper adjustment. The qualified prediction register stores 18 bits, while the issue packet and recovery and retry paths preserve the full addressing range.\n\nThe older MEM branch has priority on recovery and on the single predictor-training port. The corrected request bypasses the stale FIFO while the read pointer rebases to the current write position. Registered recovery masks younger entries and cancels speculative divider state. MEM holds a missed branch's retirement record through the corrected request, then presents it once through the existing single-channel MEM/WB retirement interface.\n\n## Timing changes\n\nThe first completion-lane implementation placed at a median of 84.107 MHz. Its limiting paths included the two dependent radix-4 trials in the narrow-divisor recurrence and the EX forwarding path. Narrow divides now use parallel radix-16 trials per work cycle and register the \n…[3319 of 6391 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:df4e430b9f149cd5]…\n and all four ISS programs. Each ISS program ran with ready buses and with both stalls enabled. The cosim Make target needed `NRET=1` for the bench port set.\n\nBoth-stall CoreMark completed in 3,871,366 marker cycles, up 0.086% from the champion, with no out-of-bounds accesses. The seed, list, matrix, state, and final CRCs were `e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a`, and `fcaf`. UART reported “Correct operation validated.”\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 87.453, 93.185, and 93.185 MHz. The median yields 240.70 CoreMark iterations/s and falls short of the 103 MHz target. Fetch recovery limited option 0. Forwarding into address generation limited options 1 and 2, with nearby EX ALU, divider, and branch recovery paths.\n\nThe stock single-issue formal run on the selected RTL completed 5 of 53 checks before the local deadline. Liveness, backward PC continuity, retirement uniqueness, causality, and retirement cover passed. The run reported zero failures and no counterexample. The full gate remains unverified.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
