{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"micro_opt\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; [print(d[\\\"id\\\"], d[\\\"title\\\"], d.get(\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"), d.get(\\\"seeds\\\"), d.get(\\\"lesson\\\",\\\"\\\")) for l in sys.stdin for d in [json.loads(l)]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-00eedbd Baseline retest for cores/bench 5.442 [5.14, 5.442, 5.442] \nhyp-20261122-001-r1s0 Fold DIV and REM into a shared radix-4 unit 39.19 [39.625, 39.19, 39.19] - 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +619.88%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM on the shared EX path with registered radix-4 rounds raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 39.190 MHz, with four cascaded steps per round setting the new timing limit and CoreMark's divide-free marker window staying at 4,491,485 cycles.\nhyp-20261122-001-r2s0 Use valid-only bubbles in the fetch and decode stages 47.56 [45.74, 47.56, 47.56] - 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.37%): Using valid-only fetch and decode bubbles removes the fetch NOP mux and ID/EX payload clear from redirect feedback, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the EX-to-dmem path and raising median Fmax from 45.134 to 47.560 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\nhyp-20261122-001-r3s0 Register RV32M partial products before accumulation 53.987 [52.803, 53.987, 53.987] - 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 (regression, +7.70%): Registering the shared RV32M multiplier's four 17-by-17 partial products before carry-save accumulation shifted the Gowin timing limit from the EX ALU to the divider, and the median Fmax gain from 49.042 to 53.987 MHz outweighed the 2.21% increase in CoreMark marker cycles from one extra EX cycle per multiply.\nhyp-20261122-001-r4s0 Replace serial divider launch trials with a nibble lookup 59.974 [55.989, 59.974, 59.974] - 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r4s0 (regression, +9.30%): Replacing two dependent radix-4 launch trials with a one-hot nibble lookup for small divisors and computing operand magnitudes in parallel slices shifted the Gowin timing limit to the EX forwarding and redirect path to the fetch PC, raising median Fmax from 54.869 to 59.974 MHz at the same 4,233,123 CoreMark marker cycles.\nhyp-20261122-001-r5s0 Register operand data and bypass matches together in Decode 75.404 [75.331, 75.404, 75.404] - 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r5s0 (regression, \n…[2493 of 5565 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a7609fb00045c93a]…\n to ID valid, which capped the Gowin median at 89.786 MHz against the 90.333 MHz needed for promotion at the unchanged CoreMark marker cycle count.\nhyp-20261122-001-r11s0 Share aligned divider trials across both radices 91.817 [81.954, 91.817, 91.817] - 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r11s0 (regression, -5.12%): Sharing radix-8 and radix-16 trial subtractors saved 143 Gowin logic cells, with narrow remainder feedback capping median Fmax at 91.817 MHz even with a bit-15 borrow tap and reducing throughput in CoreMark's divide-free marker window.\nhyp-20261122-001-r12s0 Predecode divider magnitude carries on the launch edge 94.983 [90.435, 94.983, 94.983] - 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r12s0 (regression, -1.85%): Registering the low 28 dividend bits’ nonzero flag and the one-hot divisor row at launch moved divider initialization out of Gowin’s 25 worst setup paths, while ID-to-EX load/store address generation in two placements capped median Fmax at 94.983 MHz versus 96.774 MHz at the same CoreMark marker cycle count.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
