{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261122-001-r14s0|hyp-20261122-002-r14s1|hyp-20261122-002-r10s1|\\\"title\\\".*(ALU|execute)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; rows=[json.loads(l) for l in sys.stdin]; [print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"],r.get(\\\"perturbations\\\"),r.get(\\\"critical_path\\\"),r.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\")) for r in rows if r.get(\\\"id\\\") in (\\\"hyp-20261122-001-r14s0\\\",\\\"hyp-20261122-002-r10s1\\\")]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r10s1 Execute late-result consumers in a parallel MEM lane [[0, 87.12, 12], [1, 96.774, 12], [2, 96.774, 12]] {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.mem_addr_26_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.478, 'arrival_ns': 16.773, 'logic_cells': 21, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex']} # MEM completion lane\n\nFetch predecodes eligibility for BEQ, BNE, ADD, and ADDI and carries the flag with the issue packet. Packets at the last instruction-aperture word always use the ordinary EX route. Issue keeps the existing interlock for other consumers and captures both regular forwarding selectors and any pending encoded-source matches for eligible consumers. Deferred additions can supply the lane on the next cycle.\n\nEX captures the available operand and its ordinary fallback result, along with both forwarded operands for RVFI. EX checks memory alignment and qualifies the producer's write before its consumer enters MEM. MEM uses the registered MEM/WB result for equality or addition and handles a double match directly. ADDI retains its immediate when only its encoded rs2 matches, and RVFI still observes the producer's value for that tag. The EX ALU uses registered operation selects and one right shifter for SRL and SRA. Branch comparisons combine parallel byte comparisons.\n\nFetch registers the sequential, prediction, retry, and two recovery addresses. Direct targets use an 18-bit low-word sum and a 12-bit upper adjustment. The qualified prediction register stores 18 bits, while the issue packet and recovery and retry paths preserve the full addressing range.\n\nThe older MEM branch has priority on recovery and on the single predictor-training port. The corrected request bypasses the stale FIFO while the read pointer rebases to the current write position. Registered recovery masks younger entries and cancels speculative divider state. MEM holds a missed branch's retirement record through the corrected request, then presents it once through the existing single-channel MEM/WB retirement interface.\n\n## Timing \n…[4847 of 7919 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f5d95e1720639dd9]…\nwere made.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n`python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options` ran the vendor flow for all three placements.\n\n| Placement option | Candidate Fmax (MHz) | Champion Fmax (MHz) | Candidate limiting path |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 86.645 | 87.120 | EX/MEM ALU result through forwarding and the ALU back to EX/MEM |\n| 1 | 91.354 | 96.774 | EX/MEM ALU result through forwarding and the ALU back to EX/MEM |\n| 2 | 91.354 | 96.774 | EX/MEM ALU result through forwarding and the ALU back to EX/MEM |\n\nThe 91.354 MHz median yields 236.18 CoreMark iterations/s, down 5.60% from 250.19 and below the 101.23 MHz promotion threshold. The candidate uses 4,692 logic cells, 1,148 registers, four block RAMs and three DSPs. In the median placements, divider feedback and fetch recovery also land within 0.02 ns of the limiting ALU feedback path. The separate EX and older member-select variant measured an 84.841 MHz median with the original ALU result merge and an 83.192 MHz median with the grouped result merge.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
