{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\\\"|\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261122-003-r2s2\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | sed -E 's/\"'^[0-9]+://'\"' | python -c 'import json,sys; [print(d[\\\"id\\\"], d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"], d.get(\\\"critical_path\\\"), d.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\")) for d in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]' \"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 Retiming the ID/EX boundary around operand selection will let EX start\nwith registered bypass matches and send redirect feedback only to the PC\nand the next instruction's valid bit. Keep the existing single-issue\nschedule. In ID, compare each source against the destinations of current\nID/EX and EX/MEM, which become the next EX/MEM and MEM/WB producers on\nissue. Register both match bits per source with the operands. In EX,\nqualify the matches with actual producer write permission and give the\nyounger eligible writer priority. A load can forward from MEM/WB after\nthe existing load-use bubble, and a trapping writer is ineligible.\nOne-hot masked muxes remove tag comparison and binary select encoding\nfrom the execute path. Preserve the regfile's write-first bypass for\nthe producer already in WB when the consumer issues.\n\nHold the match bits with the consumer when an older data request stalls\nthe stages. Maintain the forwarding eligibility of a retained MEM/WB\npayload after its retirement valid bit clears. Keep the one-cycle\nload-use interlock and capture DIV/REM operands at launch before the\nproducer slots can drain. An invalid slot may carry arbitrary payload,\nso use explicit validity for operand producers, the load-use interlock,\nredirects, and data requests. Base the fetch candidate's validity on\ninstruction readiness, then apply redirect at the ID/EX valid bit\nseparately. Qualify the redirect with EX advancement and give it PC\npriority over instruction-memory backpressure. Set ID/EX valid to zero\non a redirect or load-use bubble while latching the payload, and hold\nboth validity and payload during an older memory stall or divide wait.\nThis preserves the one-cycle redirect penalty and divider schedule.\n\nA scratch prototype placed at 48.677, 49.939, and 49.939 MHz on the\nTang Nano 20K with the unchanged Gowin harness. The longest paths moved\nto the divider or the multiply result path. The prototype matched the\nchampion's 4,491,485 marker cycles and passed the CoreMark CRC gate with\nboth stall s\n…[7476 of 10548 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f617e13e862dfb27]…\nesult mux into mutually exclusive ALU, divide, and link result paths after the initial Gowin median measured 43.967 MHz. The final critical paths still run through the EX ALU to the EX/MEM register or dmem RAM. The final build used 3,416 logic cells and 765 registers.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 fast single-issue checks on both runs.\n- All 78 cocotb cases passed. Directed tests cover consecutive counter aliasing, reversals in both directions, stalled recovery, misaligned and reserved direct transfers, forwarded JALR, and the exact nine-cycle cold BEQ and eight-cycle predicted JAL divider retirement gaps.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed all five ELFs. The trace ELFs passed with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark produced the required CRCs.\n- Gowin measured 45.723, 49.042, and 49.042 MHz. The median of 49.042 MHz and 4,198,541 bracketed CoreMark cycles yielded 116.81 iterations/s, up 16.24% from 100.49 iterations/s.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
