{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_56", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith('r6s2'):\n  print(d['hypothesis'])\nPY\ndate -u '+%H:%M:%S UTC'\"", "aggregated_output": "{'motivation': \"The r5s1 champion spends 4,233,123 cycles in the stalled CoreMark window\\nand its Gowin timing limit is the divider recurrence. The r5s2 predictor\\nfrontend cut the same workload to 3,979,945 cycles. The older path from\\nthe register file through the EX ALU to dmem limited r5s2 to 64.667 MHz.\\nPair that frontend with the champion's registered operand selects and\\ndedicated load/store address path to recover those cycle savings at a\\nclock rate near the champion's.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Decouple the gshare request PC from decode with a four-entry flow-through\\nFIFO for the instruction, PC, direct target, applied direction and saved\\nlookup index. Use a packed-vector array sized with the width of the raw\\npacket signal, an asynchronous head read and a synchronous tail write.\\nUse three-bit head and tail pointers with wrap bits. Write the raw packet\\nto the physical tail whenever the pointers show free space so the RAM\\nwrite enable depends on registered occupancy. Move the logical tail only for\\na ready response that enters the FIFO, and move the head only when decode\\naccepts its packet. Bypass directly to decode when empty and ready. Advance\\nthe request PC on a logical enqueue or an accepted bypass. Fetch\\nmay fill while decode holds for a load-use dependency, divide or dmem stall,\\nand decode may drain during an imem stall. A full FIFO resumes accepting\\nresponses on the cycle after a pop. Use the existing direct JAL prediction,\\n64-entry gshare table and two-bit resolved history. Train once per advancing\\nconditional branch using its saved index and resolved condition.\\n\\nAt the EX boundary, register a directi