{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in {'hyp-20261122-003-r7s2','hyp-20261122-002-r7s1','hyp-20261122-001-r7s0'}:\n  print(d['id']); print(d.get('hypothesis')); print(d.get('changes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r7s0\n{'motivation': 'The 201.60 iter/s champion runs at an 85.341 MHz Gowin median over\\n4,233,123 CoreMark marker cycles. A repeat of placement 0 showed that\\nthe EX branch condition path to the fetch PC is within 0.003 ns of the\\nreported EX ALU critical path. The condition selects the resolved PC\\nand then drives fetch recovery arbitration. The direction and word\\naddress needed for recovery can be prepared separately.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Capture the alternate direct successor in ID/EX with the prediction.\\nSelect PC+4 for a predicted-taken direct transfer and the saved target\\nfor a predicted-not-taken one. In EX, use the forwarded JALR sum for a\\nJALR recovery and the saved alternate for direct recovery. Keep the\\nexisting resolved next PC for RVFI. The direction check produces the\\nmiss pulse independently of the recovery data.\\n\\nCapture the miss pulse and recovery word beside the normal fetch-PC\\nregister on the advancing EX edge. The registered pulse selects the\\nrecovery word as the following cycle's imem request and prediction PC.\\nA ready recovery response advances to its predicted successor. If\\nfetch cannot accept the response, copy the recovery word into the\\nnormal fetch PC so dropping the pulse leaves the request in place.\\nQualify the recovery word capture with the miss pulse and keep the\\nexisting same-edge squash of the younger ID/EX instruction. Reset\\nclears the pulse and the normal fetch PC. This removes the branch\\ncomparator from the normal wide fetch-PC data mux and retains the\\none-bubble ready-bus recovery. A miss waits until EX can advance if\\nan older data transaction holds it. Preserve word alignment, full-width\\naddresses, gshare indexing and training, link write suppression on\\nmisalignment, and the current load-use and divide interlocks.\\n\\nThe scratch prototype passed eight RVFI trace comparisons and ran\\ncanonical stalled CoreMark in 4,233,123 marker cycles with all CRCs\\npassing. Gowin reported 85.051, 88.508 and 88.508 MHz across the three\\nplacements. The med\n…[13736 of 16808 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3d0bc95ab386393a]…\ncovery and training.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Connect the recovery and queue-consumption controls. Mask the younger ID/EX instruction for execute and hazard logic during recovery, including all side-effect and training enables.'}, {'file': 'rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'description': 'Derive queue consumption from the existing ID/EX acceptance conditions. Preserve load-use bubbles and dmem and divide hold priority while allowing fetch runahead.'}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': 'Capture a ready recovery bypass with the normal decode acceptance rule and squash any stale held instruction on recovery. Preserve operand selections and saved prediction metadata.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Check FIFO wraparound, stall overlap, recovery retry and wrong-path side-effect suppression. Exercise queued targets crossing both aperture boundaries, queued misaligned transfers, edge fall-through, saved-index training, and the existing exact divider gaps and RVFI order checks.'}]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
