{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in {'hyp-20261122-002-r5s1','hyp-20261122-001-r5s0','hyp-20261122-001-r6s0'}:\n  print(d['id'], d['title'])\n  print(d['implementation_notes'][:6000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r5s0 Register operand data and bypass matches together in Decode\n# Decode operand forwarding\n\nDecode registers both operand values and four nonzero destination-tag matches in ID/EX. Each match aligns with the instruction that advances into EX/MEM or MEM/WB on the same edge. Execute qualifies the matches with the producers' write eligibility and gives the younger eligible producer priority. The three one-hot selects mask the operand inputs before a bitwise OR. MEM/WB forwarding uses the retained write eligibility during dmem stalls, independently of retirement validity. The existing trap handling clears write eligibility for a faulting producer.\n\nThe register file uses two 16-word asynchronous distributed-RAM banks with synchronous writes and a 32-bit written bitmap. Reset clears the bitmap. Each read port masks the two RAM outputs and the write-first value in parallel. The read selects return zero for x0 or a still-unwritten entry and give a same-cycle write priority over the bitmap. Gowin inferred 32 RAM16 cells and four block RAMs in the timed design.\n\nFetch passes the raw instruction word with readiness-derived validity. Reset, redirects, and late-result bubbles clear ID/EX validity. Backpressure holds the payload and its matches together. The existing late-result interlocks and divide operand capture continue to cover both encoded RVFI source operands.\n\nThe parallel register-file read masks are a refinement of the proposed bank selection to avoid a cascade of bank, validity, and bypass muxes. The limiting path in both median placements is inside the divider. Placement 0 is limited by a path from MEM/WB write eligibility through Execute into dmem.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator `--lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP` passed with no warnings.\n- All seven cocotb suites passed (86 cases). Directed tests cover both register-file banks and read ports, a second reset after populating all writable entries, first-write bypasses, same-destination producer priority, fallback past a trapping JALR, and ret\n…[2497 of 5569 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f31b72d8a32550c6]…\n The script used the existing single-channel wrapper and check configuration.\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All 94 cocotb cases passed, including competing writers on both operands, a WB collision held across a stalled store, trapping and squashed writers, an unready raw fetch, and signed I/S displacement boundaries.\n- Full RVFI co-simulation passed for selftest and all three random programs with ready buses and with both stall streams. Canonical stalled CoreMark passed the UART validation and all five CRC checks with no out-of-bounds accesses. Ten iterations took 4,233,123 marker cycles.\n- Gowin placements 0, 1, and 2 measured 73.504, 77.218, and 77.218 MHz. Placement 0 used 3,237 logic cells and 762 registers. The median yields 182.41 iterations/s, 24.82% above the champion. The divider recurrence limits all three placements. The measured median is 0.389 MHz below the prototype and 12.508 MHz above the tournament threshold.\n\nhyp-20261122-001-r6s0 Use byte-sliced borrow lookahead in the divider\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
