rwarded JALR target.\n\nThe registered miss pulse selects the full-width recovery word for the next imem request, PC-relative target calculation, and gshare index. A ready response advances to its predicted successor. A stalled response seeds the normal fetch PC so the request stays in place when the pulse clears. Reset clears the pulse and normal PC.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All 96 cocotb cases in seven runners passed, including gshare direction reversals, forwarded JALR, misaligned targets, and the load-use and divide interlocks. The extended rec\n…[8336 of 11408 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ada57c27d326961c]…\ndom programs matched the ISS with ready and stalled memory. Canonical stalled CoreMark passed the CRC and completion checks and took 3,979,945 marker cycles for 10 iterations.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 83.351, 87.114, and 87.114 MHz. The 87.114 MHz median yields 218.88 iterations/s, below the 90.33 MHz promotion threshold at this marker count. The longest path in all three placements ends at the registered EX ALU result. For options 1 and 2, the redirect and divider paths are within 0.002 ns of the ALU path.\n\nYosys equivalence checks against the champion passed for the fetch and execute stages. An additional bounded SAT check passed for fetch starting from reset. The stock single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` run was stopped near the end of the implementation budget with 8 of 53 checks complete, all passing. These included cover, liveness, illegal instruction, order uniqueness, backward PC, causality, ADDI, and AND. The run reported no counterexample or check failure before it was stopped.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
