{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261122-(002-r(6|7|8|9)s1|001-r9s0)\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; ds=[json.loads(l) for l in sys.stdin]; [print(x[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"fitness\\\",x.get(\\\"fitness\\\"),\\\"cycles\\\",x.get(\\\"cycles\\\"),\\\"perturbations\\\",str(x.get(\\\"perturbations\\\"))[:3000], \\\"\\\\n\\\", (x.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\") or \\\"\\\")[:3500]) for x in ds]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r6s1 fitness 201.6 cycles 4233123 perturbations [[0, 84.956, 14], [1, 85.341, 12], [2, 85.341, 12]] \n \nhyp-20261122-002-r7s1 fitness 216.99 cycles 3979945 perturbations [[0, 87.942, 15], [1, 86.362, 14], [2, 86.362, 14]] \n # Decoded issue FIFO\n\nFetch now runs the existing decoder and immediate generator alongside target generation. A four-entry asynchronous-read FIFO stores the raw instruction, decoded control, full immediate, PC, and prediction metadata. Empty-queue bypass advances both wrap-bit pointers. A full queue reopens fetch on the cycle after a pop. ID reads the selected instruction's encoded register tags and captures data and forwarding selections when scheduling that packet. Load and multiply dependencies insert a bubble without removing the head, while an EX hold leaves ID/EX in place and permits fetch to fill available slots.\n\nThe advancing EX instruction supplies the redirect and preselects the opposite path from the saved prediction. The direct redirect clears the FIFO and ID/EX validity. Fetch captures the redirect pulse and alternate address in parallel so its output changes to the corrected request on that same edge, including during imem backpressure. If the corrected request stalls, fetch copies the address into its normal request register for retry.\n\nA sequential response accepted at `0x000ffffc` carries an aperture tag. Fetch holds that address and inhibits further acceptance until the tagged instruction advances in EX or an older instruction redirects. If the tagged instruction resolves sequentially, the request advances to its fall-through address.\n\nThe FIFO RAM and ID/EX payload may capture a ready younger packet on a redirect edge. The same edge clears both FIFO pointers and ID/EX validity, so the captured data is unreachable. This keeps the late EX redirect off the wide data enables.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- The placement options reached 87.942, 86.362, and 86.362 MHz. At 3,979,945 CoreMark marker cycles, the 86.362 MHz median yields 216.99 iterations/s, 7.64% above t\n…[8956 of 12028 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6920aee5b1de66e7]…\nons 0, 1, and 2 were 76.008, 81.083, and 81.083 MHz. The 81.083 MHz median yields 218.11 iterations/s and falls short of the 84.38 MHz promotion threshold. The slower placement was limited by fetch prediction to the next request and slot lookup to reference validity. The other two placements were limited by EX recovery to the slot register, with the divider close behind.\n- The immediate/target field reduced the area of the packet RAM and head. A further split of the fetch target adder preserved the marker count and lowered the measured frequency, so the submitted RTL keeps the original target adder. Bitwuzla confirmed equality and signed and unsigned ordering for the submitted byte comparator over all 32-bit operands.\n- The local single-channel formal run was incomplete within the implementation window. Six of 53 checks finished and passed, including liveness, uniqueness, illegal-instruction trapping, cover, causal ordering, and backward PC consistency. No counterexample was reported before the run stopped.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
