{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261122-002-r[12345]s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [print(x[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"cycles\\\", x[\\\"cycles\\\"],\\\"fmax\\\",x[\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",str(x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"])[:1800]) for x in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 cycles 4491485 fmax 45.134 \n {'motivation': 'The bench baseline runs at 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path spans\\nforwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path.\\nThe signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are the likeliest\\nsource of that depth. Their combinational results share the execute result\\nmux with ordinary ALU operations and memory addresses. Moving division to\\na registered unit should shorten the clock period for every instruction.\\nThe baseline Verilator trace with both bus stall flags retired two DIVU\\ninstructions in the full CoreMark run and zero DIV/REM instructions in\\nthe 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles, so the timed loop has no direct\\ndivide latency cost.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Use one blocking, registered divider for DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU. On\\nissue, capture forwarded operands and form unsigned magnitudes. Register\\nthe divisor magnitude D and the 34-bit multiple 3D, using a shift for 2D.\\nProcess the 32-bit dividend magnitude with four leading zeros in six\\ncycles of three radix-4 restoring steps. For each step, append two input\\nbits to the current remainder, subtract D, 2D, and 3D in parallel with\\n35-bit borrow detection, and select the largest multiple that fits, or\\nzero. That choice yields the next two quotient bits and the new\\nremainder. Register the running remainder and quotient between cycles.\\nApply the quotient or remainder sign from the registered final state.\\nReturn the original dividend for remainder by zero and all ones for\\ndivision by zero. Preserve signed INT_MIN / -1 and its zero remainder.\\nKeep the same launch, wait, and acceptance schedule for edge cases and\\nunder RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, using the existing four substitute results in\\nthe formal configuration.\\n\\nLaunch only when the divide is valid in EX and the ol\nhyp-20261122-002-r2s1 cycles 4491485 fmax 49.189 \n {'motivation': 'The registered divider moved the timing limit to the main pipeline at\\n45.134 MHz. Two of the three Gowin placement options repor\n…[6188 of 9260 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:658af73fcbda7bdd]…\n next-stage write eligibility. Also compare the\\nsources with the current WB destination when it is writing. Snapshot\\nthat WB value in a shared 32-bit register on the same ID/EX capture\\nedge. Register four one-hot bits for each source in ID/EX. In priority\\norder, select the eligible fast EX writer, eligible MEM writer,\\nsnapshotted WB collision, or raw register-file read. Use a single\\nmasked OR mux per operand in EX. An x0 source selects zero. Invalid\\nentries and trapping writers are ineligible, including misaligned\\njumps in EX and misaligned loads in MEM. Keep architectural reset\\nand the standalone register-file write-first mode. Use its raw read\\nmode in core so the captured WB source supplies write-first behavior.\\n\\nFeed a dedicated load/store address adder from the selected rs1 and\\nthe decoded I/S displacement. Sum the low 12 bits with carry out\\nand compute base[31:12] plus one and minus one in parallel.\\nIncrement the upper word for a nonnegative offset with carry and\\ndecrement it for a negat\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
