{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r10s1.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-002-r10s1\ntitle: \"Execute late-result consumers in a parallel MEM lane\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion spends 3,979,945 cycles in the stalled CoreMark marker window.\n  A Verilator retirement trace found 135,320 adjacent load-to-branch\n  dependencies and 32,400 adjacent multiply-to-integer-register-operation\n  dependencies. The current issue interlock can leave EX empty while the\n  producer completes in MEM. The dependent instruction can instead spend\n  that cycle collecting its other operand and finish from registered data.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add an integer completion lane beside the data-memory path in MEM. Issue\n  a conditional branch or a basic integer add, subtract, or bitwise\n  operation even when either encoded source matches a valid late EX\n  producer. Reserve the lane for these consumers and capture the operands\n  which are already available, including their architectural fallback\n  values. On the producer's MEM handoff, record which source matches an\n  eligible write. The producer's registered MEM/WB result supplies those\n  sources in the following cycle. There is no combinational path from\n  dmem read data into the late branch or arithmetic operation.\n\n  A deferred arithmetic result is eligible for normal MEM/WB forwarding.\n  Treat that instruction as a late producer for the next issue decision so\n  supported consumers can use the same lane on consecutive cycles. Let\n  addresses, shifts, indirect jumps and RV32M continue to use the existing\n  EX path and interlock. Independent instructions and branches keep their\n  existing EX timing. Hold the younger operation and its operand selectors\n  while an older data request stalls. Keep true and encoded-only operands\n  precise for RVFI, including x0 and fallback after an alignment trap.\n\n  Resolve a deferred branch while the next instruction is in EX. A correct\n  prediction allows that younger instruction to advance. A miss takes\n  precedence over any younger EX recovery, kills its outgoing valid bit\n  and cancels its issue and \n…[1473 of 4545 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:530ba71c3e389093]…\n separately for either encoded operand.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Permit lane-eligible late consumers to issue and maintain blocking interlocks for the remaining RAW cases.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Collect available operands and branch metadata for deferred execution, preserve them across stalls, and qualify younger validity and divide launch against older recovery.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Finish deferred consumers with the registered MEM/WB value, resolve branch alignment and next PC, and forward valid arithmetic results through the normal writeback path.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Accept recovery and aperture release from the oldest resolving branch and train the original saved prediction entry.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Cover late result forwarding, correct and incorrect dependent predictions, producer traps, stalled memory, wrong-path side effects, aperture ownership and single in-order retirement.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
