{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_48", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads, open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r10s2','r9s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],r.get('implementation_notes'),sep='\\\\n')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r9s2\nReplay sequential instructions from trained loops at the FIFO tail\nTrain a loop stream predictor from advancing legal conditional branches\nthat resolve taken to an aligned lower PC in the 1 MiB instruction aperture.\nRegister the EX observation and qualify loops spanning at most 64 words,\ncounting both endpoints. Keep the most recently trained interval for fill.\nFill a 64-entry direct-mapped table from accepted ready imem responses in\nthat interval. Index by PC[7:2] and store PC[19:8] with one valid bit per\nentry. Store the instruction, decoded control and full immediate from\nthe live decoder. A descriptor change leaves the tagged words resident.\nAllocate only words with a sequential architectural successor\nunder the existing decoder, excluding legal conditional branches, JAL,\nJALR and FENCE.I. Imem supplies all control transfers and the existing\ngshare receives their original fetch-time indices and EX outcomes.\n\nIn a stalled imem cycle with FIFO space, an in-aperture tag hit at the\nordinary request PC can enqueue a decoded replay packet and advance the\nrequest to PC+4. Copy the stored decoded fields, attach the current PC\nand mark the packet sequential. Feed ID from the\nregistered FIFO head when nonempty and keep the existing live-response\nbypass when empty. Give a ready response priority, and qualify logical\nFIFO tail movement with accepted live or replay traffic. The physical\npayload write can depend on registered space alone because a tentative\nwrite is unreadable until its tail advances. Inhibit replay during the\nfirst recovery request and at the last aperture word. Preserve EX's\nsame-edge squash and the live aperture hold, full-width recovery retry,\nand one-bubble recovery when imem is ready.\n\nRegister fall-through, retry and the 18-bit in-aperture live target in\nparallel with one-hot steering. Keep the registered EX recovery address\nand give its steering priority. Split the live target addition into an\n18-bit word sum and a 12-bit upper adjustment from the word carry and\ndisplacement s\n…[3762 of 6834 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:68c2aae8b5b30364]…\ner adjustment. Keep the exact\nfull-width target and alignment bit in the live packet for EX.\n\nA scratch build with these changes passed stalled CoreMark CRC\nvalidation in 3,734,625 marker cycles and passed the existing ISS\ncomparisons. Gowin measured 83.037, 88.948 and 88.948 MHz with 5,246\nlogic cells. The 88.948 MHz median yields 238.17 iterations/s, up 9.76%\nfrom 216.99. At this cycle count, promotion requires more than 84.765\nMHz. The divider recurrence set the timing limit in all three\nplacements. A local induction check proved ready-bus fetch equivalence\nto the champion, and a transition check proved the held-or-sequential\nrequest condition for staged lookups. Directed cocotb cases passed for\nconsecutive replay across a bank row wrap, tag aliases, FIFO pressure\nand code replacement across FENCE.I. Seven of 53 single-issue formal\nchecks passed within the local run budget. Recheck all placements,\ncomplete the existing single-issue formal gate, and exercise stalled\nrecovery and speculative fence squash.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
