{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_39", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261122-002-r11s1|hyp-20261122-003-r12s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json, sys; [print(x[\\\"id\\\"], x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"], x[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\") for x in map(json.loads,sys.stdin) if x[\\\"id\\\"] in (\\\"hyp-20261122-002-r11s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20261122-003-r12s2\\\")]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r11s1\nSplit the 18-bit in-aperture word sum between byte-address bits 7 and 8.\nCompute the low six-bit sum and the zero-carry middle twelve-bit sum\nindependently. Precompute the middle increment toggle mask from prefix\nANDs of middle PC XOR middle displacement. An incoming carry flips\nbit i precisely when the two operands are complementary below i.\nRegister both sums, the middle toggle mask and the low carry in the\npredicted-address lane. In the request cycle, XOR the carry-qualified\nmask into the middle sum in parallel with the other request lanes.\nA taken prediction still requests its target on the next cycle.\n\nFor aperture qualification, combine middle generate with middle\npropagate AND low carry. Check whether the upper PC plus this carry\nminus the displacement sign is zero modulo 4096 using a truth table\nover the upper PC being zero, one or all ones. Apply the existing\ntarget alignment and gshare checks on the acceptance cycle. Carry\nthe split target and its adjustment metadata through the FIFO and\nID/EX, and form the full architectural target in EX in parallel with\nbranch comparison. This includes the original target alignment bit and\nall 32 address bits for RVFI and redirect. Preserve older MEM recovery\npriority, the last-word aperture hold and the late branch lane.\n\nA bit-vector check proved the split sum and aperture test equivalent to\nfull-width addition. The six-bit scratch split passed the ISA traces\nand completed CRC-validated CoreMark in 3,868,036 marker cycles. Its\nGowin timings were 88.375, 94.328 and 94.328 MHz, yielding 243.87\niterations/s. EX forwarding into the ALU set the median, with the divider\nrecurrence close behind. Promotion needs a median above 101.23 MHz at\nthis cycle count. The 103 MHz target, about 266 iterations/s, depends on\nclearing those placement limits. Run all three placements and the stock\nsingle-retire formal, cosim and stalled CoreMark gates on implementation.\n\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r12s2\nGive live fetch and predicted loop replay independent payload banks behi\n…[2812 of 5884 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b6377de35e3e76a8]…\nrecovery request and let the corrected\nlive response bypass on a ready bus. Preserve exact live direct targets,\nfull-width recovery and retries, the last-word hold, and the ready-bus\nretirement schedule.\n\nAt 3,549,156 cycles, a 94.5 MHz Gowin median would yield 266.26 iter/sec,\nabout 6.4% above the 250.19 champion. This is a projection from r11s2's\nmeasured cycle count. Verify unchanged ready-bus issue timing and compare\nstalled marker cycles and the three Gowin placements. Check paths into\nthe bank write inputs, source-bit registers, and ID/EX issue inputs to\nestablish whether the write-path savings survive dequeue. An abstract\nqueue model matched a reference FIFO across 5,952 pointer, source, and\ncontrol transitions. Exercise alternating producers across pointer wrap,\nbypass with an empty queue, a full queue, coincident recovery and a stalled\nrequest, and FENCE.I across replay fills in RTL. Keep the existing formal,\ncosim, and CoreMark CRC gates, including the ready-bus divider gap after\nbranch recovery.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
