{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_90", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r14s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261122-002-r14s1\ntitle: \\\"Capture fast producer values on the consumer's issue edge\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 250.19 CoreMark iterations/s at a 96.774 MHz\n  Gowin median and 3,868,036 marker cycles. Round 13s1 moved address\n  generation out of the worst setup paths and exposed the recurrence\n  from the registered EX/MEM result through operand selection and\n  the ALU. In a local split-result prototype, giving ADD a separate\n  EX/MEM bank preserved marker cycles but widened the bypass network\n  and moved the median limit to branch comparison at 92.017 MHz.\n  The fast producer and the issuing consumer already share an edge.\n  Capturing the produced value directly in the consumer's operand\n  registers can shorten both the ALU and address operand paths.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move fast EX forwarding onto the data inputs of the ID/EX operand\n  registers. On an issue edge, select the current EX result for an\n  eligible youngest matching writer. Otherwise capture the register\n  file's write-first value or zero if a registered MEM/WB bypass will\n  supply the operand or the encoded source is x0. Keep the MEM/WB\n  select registered with the operand. EX combines its captured value\n  with just that qualified MEM/WB input. Both encoded tags still\n  participate in the same hazard and forwarding priority for RVFI.\n+\n  Fuse the fast forwarding selection with the EX result merge for\n  ADD and ADDI. Expose the unmasked ordinary sum and the merged\n  remaining fast results from EX. During issue compute separate\n  one-hot selects for the sum, the other fast result, and the\n  register-file value. Apply them in one masked OR on each ID/EX\n  operand input. Use the same producer result for ordinary EX/MEM\n  writeback. Issue also captures the ALU's immediate-or-register\n  operand B and its qualified MEM/WB select, so EX can consume it\n  directly. Retain the encoded rs2 operand separately for stores,\n  branches, RV32M, and RVFI.\n+\n  Qualify the EX writer with validity and the existing jump alignment\n  check. Keep load, RV32M and deferred ADD results on the existing\n  MEM/WB bypass or late completion path. An invalid or trapping\n  producer falls through to the next eligible source. Hold operand\n  snapshots and MEM/WB selects together during a dmem stall or divide\n  wait. Keep registered recovery priority so a captured wrong-path\n  operand cannot reach an architectural write or a data request. The\n  issue, divide handoff and single-channel retirement schedules stay\n  cycle-equivalent, including on ready buses.\n+\n  A local prototype that captured the merged EX value passed canonical\n  stalled CoreMark in 3,868,036 marker cycles. At that cycle count,\n  promotion requires above 101.23 MHz. The fused selection targets\n  103 MHz, about 266 iterations/s. Inspect the new ALU-to-ID/EX path\n  as well as the forwarding-to-address, branch recovery and divider\n  paths in all three Gowin placements. Verify the full stagnant and\n  advancing pipeline cases with stock cosim and single-channel formal,\n  including liveness, and compare the stalled CoreMark cycles and CRCs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \\\"Define the per-operand ID/EX snapshots and MEM/WB selects, including the ALU B payload.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Preserve youngest eligible tag matching and qualify the two fast result sources for each issuing operand.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Select and capture the current EX result or write-first register data on issue, with a dedicated immediate-or-register ALU B snapshot.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \\\"Expose the core sum separately from the other ALU results for the fused issue selection while retaining the standalone ALU interface behavior.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Export fast result lanes and consume the captured operands with only the registered MEM/WB bypass. Preserve writeback and late completion routing.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Feed fast values back to the issue snapshots and preserve stage hold, producer qualification, recovery and RVFI wiring.\\\"\nYAML\nsed -i 's/\"'^+$//'\"' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r14s1.yaml\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport jsonschema\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r14s1.yaml')\ndata=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.Draft7Validator(json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text())).validate(data)\nassert data['id']==p.stem and data['category']=='structural'\nassert type(data['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nassert all((Path('cores/bench') / c['file']).is_file() for c in data['changes'])\nprint(f'Validated {p}')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
