{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nentries=[json.loads(l) for l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\nfor d in entries:\n if d['id'] in ('hyp-20261122-002-r10s1','hyp-20261122-002-r14s1'):\n  print(d['id'], 'timing:',d['critical_path'],'seeds:',str(d['seeds'])[:3000])\n  print(d['implementation_notes'][:4000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r10s1 timing: {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.mem_addr_26_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.478, 'arrival_ns': 16.773, 'logic_cells': 21, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex']} seeds: [87.12, 96.774, 96.774]\n# MEM completion lane\n\nFetch predecodes eligibility for BEQ, BNE, ADD, and ADDI and carries the flag with the issue packet. Packets at the last instruction-aperture word always use the ordinary EX route. Issue keeps the existing interlock for other consumers and captures both regular forwarding selectors and any pending encoded-source matches for eligible consumers. Deferred additions can supply the lane on the next cycle.\n\nEX captures the available operand and its ordinary fallback result, along with both forwarded operands for RVFI. EX checks memory alignment and qualifies the producer's write before its consumer enters MEM. MEM uses the registered MEM/WB result for equality or addition and handles a double match directly. ADDI retains its immediate when only its encoded rs2 matches, and RVFI still observes the producer's value for that tag. The EX ALU uses registered operation selects and one right shifter for SRL and SRA. Branch comparisons combine parallel byte comparisons.\n\nFetch registers the sequential, prediction, retry, and two recovery addresses. Direct targets use an 18-bit low-word sum and a 12-bit upper adjustment. The qualified prediction register stores 18 bits, while the issue packet and recovery and retry paths preserve the full addressing range.\n\nThe older MEM branch has priority on recovery and on the single predictor-training port. The corrected request bypasses the stale FIFO while the read pointer rebases to the current write position. Registered recovery masks younger entries and cancels speculative divider state. MEM holds a missed branch's retirement record through the corrected request, then presents it once through the existing single-channel MEM/WB retirement interface.\n\n## Timing changes\n\nThe first completion-lane implementation placed at a med\n…[4671 of 7743 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9f46f9107cce3e68]…\nEM recovery to ALU B capture | 11.233 | Outside worst 25 | 11.244 | 11.236 |\n\nThe 88.933 MHz median implies 229.92 stalled CoreMark iterations/s at the measured cycle count. This is below the champion’s 96.774 MHz median and the 101.23 MHz promotion threshold. The original fused RTL measured 77.814 MHz. Explicit mux boundaries, registered eligibility and FIFO emptiness raised the median. Enabling snapshots during all cycles free of EX holds measured 82.576 MHz, so the delivered RTL uses issue-qualified capture.\n\nThe initial formal invocation stopped at Yosys parsing `$bits` with a typedef argument. Replacing that expression with a typed mux fixed elaboration. The unchanged `formal/run_all.sh` auto-selected the single-channel wrapper and generated 53 checks for the delivered RTL. Six passed within the local time budget, including liveness, uniqueness, backward PC, causality, illegal-instruction behavior and cover. The full suite remained incomplete with 47 unfinished checks and zero reported counterexamples.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
