{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261122-002-r1s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(*[(k,v) for k,v in d.items() if k not in (\\\"changes\\\",)],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")'\"", "aggregated_output": "('id', 'hyp-20261122-002-r1s1')\n('title', 'Share a multicycle divider across RV32M divide and remainder')\n('category', 'structural')\n('motivation', 'The bench baseline runs at 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path spans\\nforwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path.\\nThe signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are the likeliest\\nsource of that depth. Their combinational results share the execute result\\nmux with ordinary ALU operations and memory addresses. Moving division to\\na registered unit should shorten the clock period for every instruction.\\nThe baseline Verilator trace with both bus stall flags retired two DIVU\\ninstructions in the full CoreMark run and zero DIV/REM instructions in\\nthe 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles, so the timed loop has no direct\\ndivide latency cost.\\n')\n('hypothesis', {'motivation': 'The bench baseline runs at 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path spans\\nforwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path.\\nThe signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are the likeliest\\nsource of that depth. Their combinational results share the execute result\\nmux with ordinary ALU operations and memory addresses. Moving division to\\na registered unit should shorten the clock period for every instruction.\\nThe baseline Verilator trace with both bus stall flags retired two DIVU\\ninstructions in the full CoreMark run and zero DIV/REM instructions in\\nthe 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles, so the timed loop has no direct\\ndivide latency cost.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Use one blocking, registered divider for DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU. On\\nissue, capture forwarded operands and form unsigned magnitudes. Register\\nthe divisor magnitude D and the 34-bit multiple 3D, using a shift for 2D.\\nProcess the 32-bit dividend magnitude with four leading zeros in six\\ncycles of three radix-4 restoring steps. For each step, append two input\\nbits to the current remainder, subtract D, 2D, and 3D in parallel with\\n35-bit borrow detection, and select the largest m\n…[7061 of 10133 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b2e74258b5f73d11]…\nted 42.055, 45.134, and 45.134 MHz. The worst reported setup path was in option 0, from `cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0` through ID and the execute ALU to `dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s` in 15 logic cells. Options 1 and 2 reported a 27.450 ns worst arrival on the redirect feedback path to ID/EX. Gowin reported 3,009 logic cells and 598 registers for option 0, compared with 12,312 cells and 460 registers in the baseline.\\n\\nMeasured fitness increased by **729.1%**, exceeding the 4.6% requirement. Both measurements used ten iterations and the same marker-bracketed cycle count.\\n')\n('timestamp', '2026-11-22T02:49:33.850765Z')\n('slot', 1)\n('round_id', 1)\n('lesson', '- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +729.13%): Reducing the shared divider from four cascaded radix-4 steps per cycle to three moved the critical path out of the divider and raised median Gowin Fmax from 39.190 to 45.134 MHz, with a nine-cycle retirement gap after a redirect or load-use bubble on ready buses still within the ten-cycle liveness bound.')\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
