{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_42", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r2s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\ntitle: Retiming bypass selection across the decode-to-execute boundary\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The registered divider moved the timing limit to the main pipeline at\n  45.134 MHz. Two of the three Gowin placement options reported their\n  longest path from the register file through EX branch resolution, the\n  fetch instruction mux, and the load-use interlock back into ID/EX. The\n  path through operand delivery and the multiply result mux to data memory\n  is nearly as long. EX currently compares register tags and encodes a\n  priority before its ALU and branch comparator can use either operand.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retiming the ID/EX boundary around operand selection will let EX start\n  with registered bypass matches and send redirect feedback only to the PC\n  and the next instruction's valid bit. Keep the existing single-issue\n  schedule. In ID, compare each source against the destinations of current\n  ID/EX and EX/MEM, which become the next EX/MEM and MEM/WB producers on\n  issue. Register both match bits per source with the operands. In EX,\n  qualify the matches with actual producer write permission and give the\n  younger eligible writer priority. A load can forward from MEM/WB after\n  the existing load-use bubble, and a trapping writer is ineligible.\n  Feed the operands through one-hot\n  masked muxes so register comparison and binary select encoding leave\n  the execute path. Preserve the regfile's write-first bypass for the\n  producer already in WB when the consumer issues.\n\n  Hold the match bits with the consumer when an older data request stalls\n  the stages. Maintain the forwarding eligibility of a retained MEM/WB\n  payload after its retirement valid bit clears. Keep the one-cycle\n  load-use interlock and capture DIV/REM operands at launch before the\n  producer slots can drain. An invalid slot may carry arbitrary payload,\n  so use explicit validity for operand producers, the load-use interlock,\n  redirects, and data requests. Base the fetch candidate's validity on\n  instruction\n…[2032 of 5104 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bf3a5461f80730ed]…\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Qualify the existing load-use interlock with valid instructions, separate bubbles from holds, and preserve an older data request's priority over EX advancement.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Use one-hot operand muxes driven by the registered matches and producer eligibility. Redirect only from a valid advancing instruction. Preserve alignment traps and the existing DIV/REM launch, result, and operand-capture schedule.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Gate bus effects on EX/MEM validity and preserve the independently valid MEM/WB forwarding payload across a data stall. Keep retirement, alignment traps, and load extension correct.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Exercise two producers with the same destination, including a trapping younger writer, a load-use bubble, forwarding across a prolonged data stall, and taken redirection into instruction backpressure. Check consecutive RVFI order and absence of wrong-path bus writes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
