{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|multiply-result|hyp-20261122-003-r2s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-900; wc -l cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl; git log -7 --oneline\"","aggregated_output":"3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Share a multicycle divider across RV32M divide and remainder\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline runs at 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path spans\\nforwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path.\\nThe signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are the likeliest\\nsource of that depth. Their combinational results share the execute result\\nmux with ordinary ALU operations and memory addresses. Moving division to\\na registered unit should shorten the clock period for every instruction.\\nThe baseline Verilator trace with both bus stall flags retired two DIVU\\ninstructions in the full CoreMark run and zero DIV/REM instructions in\\nthe 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles, so the timed loop has no direct\\ndivide latency cost.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench baseline runs at 5.442 MHz. \n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Retiming bypass selection across the decode-to-execute boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The registered divider moved the timing limit to the main pipeline at\\n45.134 MHz. Two of the three Gowin placement options reported their\\nlongest path from the register file through EX branch resolution, the\\nfetch instruction mux, and the load-use interlock back into ID/EX. The\\npath through operand delivery and the multiply result mux to data memory\\nis nearly as long. EX currently compares register tags and encodes a\\npriority before its ALU and branch comparator can use either operand.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The registered divider moved the timing limit to the main pipeline at\\n45.134 MHz. Two of the three Gowin placement options reported their\\nlongest path from the register file through EX branch resolution, the\\nfetch i\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Fetch-time direct targets with a 64-entry gshare predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench champion loses a fetch slot on each taken control\\ntransfer. The previous 16-entry tagged BTB saved 5.73% of CoreMark cycles,\\nbut its Fmax loss left a 3.96% fitness gain. In the champion's canonical\\nCoreMark trace, conditional branches and JAL account for 96% of taken\\ntransfers. Their targets are available from the fetched instruction. A\\nsmall direction table can capture most of the benefit while reducing EX\\nrecovery for direct transfers to a direction check.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench champion loses a fetch slot on each taken control\\ntransfer. The previous 16-entry tagged BTB saved 5.73% of CoreMark cycles,\\nbut its Fmax loss left a 3.96% fitness gain. In the champion's canonical\\nCoreMark trace,\n7 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\nc3591ab log: hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 improvement\nfcb210f log: hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 regression\n88b08d7 log: hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 regression\n6d498e4 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2: Fetch-time direct targets with a 64-entry gshare predictor (+16.2%)\n8639935 log: hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 regression\n41e2f0a log: hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 improvement\n8b036ea log: hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 regression\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
