{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"nl -ba cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r3s1.yaml | sed -n '1,140p'\"", "aggregated_output": "     1\tid: hyp-20261122-002-r3s1\n     2\ttitle: \"Move RV32M result completion to the memory stage\"\n     3\tcategory: structural\n     4\tmotivation: |\n     5\t  The bench champion scored 116.81 iterations/s at a 49.042 MHz Gowin\n     6\t  median and 4,198,541 marker cycles. Its worst reported path crosses\n     7\t  the execute ALU into EX/MEM, and other placements end in dmem RAM.\n     8\t  Registering bypass comparisons in the previous structural candidate\n     9\t  left the multiply-result path to dmem as the timing limit. Moving\n    10\t  multiply completion to MEM in a scratch prototype shifted the limit\n    11\t  to the divider's three cascaded radix-4 steps. A common MEM return\n    12\t  point for RV32M gives the divider another work edge and takes both\n    13\t  units out of the EX result mux that feeds the data-address path.\n    14\thypothesis: |\n    15\t  Route all RV32M operations through a common late-result path. Keep\n    16\t  RV32I ALU results, jump links, and data addresses on the existing EX\n    17\t  path. Make MEM/WB the first stage that can forward a completed M\n    18\t  result, using the same registered writeback value as loads and RV32I.\n    19\t\n    20\t  For multiplication, split each forwarded operand into two 16-bit\n    21\t  limbs in EX. Zero-extend the low limbs to 17 bits. Sign-extend the high\n    22\t  A limb for MULH and MULHSU, and the high B limb for MULH. Zero-extend\n    23\t  the remaining high limbs. Generate four parallel signed 17-by-17\n    24\t  partial products sized for Gowin DSP inference. Register the partials\n    25\t  and the half-select when the multiply enters EX/MEM.\n    26\t\n    27\t  In MEM combine the registered products at shifts 0, 16, 16, and 32.\n    28\t  Sign-extend the cross products before shifting and reduce the weighted\n    29\t  terms modulo 2^64 with two carry-save reductions and one 48-bit\n    30\t  carry-propagate add. The lowest 16 product bits come directly from\n    31\t  the low-by-low product. Select the low word for MUL and the high word\n    32\t  for MULH, MULHSU, and MULHU. \n…[4894 of 7966 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b17363674fede8a4]…\nsv\n    98\t    description: \"Apply the encoded-source interlock to M operations. Honor older data holds and defer the post-divide bubble until the divide can hand off.\"\n    99\t  - file: test/test_alu.py\n   100\t    description: \"Retain the RV32I ALU checks and move the existing multiply vectors into the staged multiplier suite.\"\n   101\t  - file: test/test_multiplier.py\n   102\t    description: \"Exercise all four variants with the existing vectors plus signed and mixed-sign boundaries, random consecutive inputs, and held partial products.\"\n   103\t  - file: test/test_divider.py\n   104\t    description: \"Keep existing RV32M arithmetic vectors, extend tests around divisor magnitudes 0 through 16, and check final-edge handoff, delayed acceptance, and reset.\"\n   105\t  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n   106\t    description: \"Add the multiplier to the RTL source list. Check independent and dependent M instructions, squashed and stalled operations, exact dependency and redirect gaps, and ordered single-channel retirement.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
