{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | jq '{id,title,hypothesis,motivation,changes,eval,result,implementation_notes}'\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r1s1\",\n  \"title\": \"Share a multicycle divider across RV32M divide and remainder\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline runs at 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path spans\\nforwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path.\\nThe signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are the likeliest\\nsource of that depth. Their combinational results share the execute result\\nmux with ordinary ALU operations and memory addresses. Moving division to\\na registered unit should shorten the clock period for every instruction.\\nThe baseline Verilator trace with both bus stall flags retired two DIVU\\ninstructions in the full CoreMark run and zero DIV/REM instructions in\\nthe 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles, so the timed loop has no direct\\ndivide latency cost.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Use one blocking, registered divider for DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU. On\\nissue, capture forwarded operands and form unsigned magnitudes. Register\\nthe divisor magnitude D and the 34-bit multiple 3D, using a shift for 2D.\\nProcess the 32-bit dividend magnitude with four leading zeros in six\\ncycles of three radix-4 restoring steps. For each step, append two input\\nbits to the current remainder, subtract D, 2D, and 3D in parallel with\\n35-bit borrow detection, and select the largest multiple that fits, or\\nzero. That choice yields the next two quotient bits and the new\\nremainder. Register the running remainder and quotient between cycles.\\nApply the quotient or remainder sign from the registered final state.\\nReturn the original dividend for remainder by zero and all ones for\\ndivision by zero. Preserve signed INT_MIN / -1 and its zero remainder.\\nKeep the same launch, wait, and acceptance schedule for edge cases and\\nunder RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, using the existing four substitute results in\\nthe formal configuration.\\n\\nLaunch only when the divide is valid in EX and the older EX/MEM entry\\ncan advance. Hold fetch and ID/EX from launch through the six steps.\\nDuring that interval, fee\n…[31000 of 34072 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9f3e7f5b0173dde7]…\n post-divide bubble until the divide can hand off.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Retain the RV32I ALU checks and move the existing multiply vectors into the staged multiplier suite.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"test/test_multiplier.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Exercise all four variants with the existing vectors plus signed and mixed-sign boundaries, random consecutive inputs, and held partial products.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"test/test_divider.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Keep existing RV32M arithmetic vectors, extend tests around divisor magnitudes 0 through 16, and check final-edge handoff, delayed acceptance, and reset.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Add the multiplier to the RTL source list. Check independent and dependent M instructions, squashed and stalled operations, exact dependency and redirect gaps, and ordered single-channel retirement.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"eval\": null,\n  \"result\": null,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
