{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_14", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n    d=json.loads(line)\n    if d['id'].endswith(('r2s1','r2s2','r3s1')):\n        print(d['id'])\n        print(d.get('implementation_notes') or '')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\n# Retiming bypass selection across the decode-to-execute boundary\n\nDecode compares both source tags against the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations. ID/EX captures the four nonzero-destination match bits with the consumer's operands. EX qualifies the matches with the producer's write eligibility and uses a one-hot masked mux for each operand. The younger eligible producer takes priority. The regfile's existing write-first bypass supplies writes already in WB when the consumer issues.\n\nFetch supplies the raw instruction and uses imem readiness for candidate validity. A redirect from advancing EX updates the PC even during imem backpressure. Redirects and load-use bubbles clear ID/EX validity while the stage captures the payload. A pending older data request or divide wait holds the consumer and its matches. MEM/WB retains forwarding eligibility and data during a data stall while clearing retirement validity. DIV/REM captures the forwarded operands at launch and retains the existing execution schedule.\n\n`forward_unit` handles ID tag matching. EX owns one-hot selection, and the load-use interlock reuses ID's comparisons against the current EX destination. MEM derives backpressure from the valid, aligned bus request, so a misaligned access can retire as a trap while dmem is stalled. The channel-0 RVFI interface follows `nret: 1` in `core.yaml`.\n\nDirected cocotb checks cover matching writers for both operands, a trapping younger writer, the exact load-use bubble, forwarding a retired load across a stalled store, and redirects with both instruction and data backpressure. They check RVFI order and bus writes.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall` and no warnings.\n- Single-channel ALTOPS formal passed all 53 checks after one fix. The first run stopped during Yosys elaboration because it could not resolve direct member assignments to the packed-struct function return. Assigning the complete return value from a local packed struct fixed elaboration. The first run produced no c\n…[1954 of 5026 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9b104e50d31be956]…\n exclusive ALU, divide, and link result paths after the initial Gowin median measured 43.967 MHz. The final critical paths still run through the EX ALU to the EX/MEM register or dmem RAM. The final build used 3,416 logic cells and 765 registers.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 fast single-issue checks on both runs.\n- All 78 cocotb cases passed. Directed tests cover consecutive counter aliasing, reversals in both directions, stalled recovery, misaligned and reserved direct transfers, forwarded JALR, and the exact nine-cycle cold BEQ and eight-cycle predicted JAL divider retirement gaps.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed all five ELFs. The trace ELFs passed with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark produced the required CRCs.\n- Gowin measured 45.723, 49.042, and 49.042 MHz. The median of 49.042 MHz and 4,198,541 bracketed CoreMark cycles yielded 116.81 iterations/s, up 16.24% from 100.49 iterations/s.\n\nhyp-20261122-002-r3s1\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
