{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r5s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-002-r5s1\ntitle: Give data memory a dedicated registered address lane\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion scores 146.14 CoreMark iterations/s at 61.864 MHz\n  over 4,233,123 marker cycles. Its worst reported Gowin path reaches\n  the data-memory address through ID, EX forwarding, and the integer ALU.\n  A load or store needs only rs1 plus its signed 12-bit displacement, but\n  the address currently passes through the general ALU operand and result\n  selection and the jump-link result mux. It also shares the result\n  register with the integer bypass network.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give loads and stores their own EX address-generation lane. Feed it\n  directly from forwarded rs1 and the decoded I/S displacement. Capture\n  the full effective byte address in a dedicated EX/MEM register and\n  drive MEM address, lane selection, alignment checks, and RVFI memory\n  address from that register. Keep the ALU and jump-link result on the\n  existing writeback and EX/MEM bypass lane. This removes the general\n  ALU selection and link mux from the memory address cone and lets Gowin\n  place the address register near the data RAM.\n\n  Exploit the signed 12-bit offset in the dedicated adder. Sum the low\n  12 base and offset bits with a carry out. In parallel, form the unchanged,\n  incremented, and decremented upper 20 bits of the base. A positive\n  offset with carry increments the upper word. A negative offset without\n  carry decrements it. In the other cases the upper word is unchanged.\n  Select and register the full modulo-2^32 sum on the EX/MEM handoff.\n\n  The two lanes advance on the same EX/MEM edge, so load latency, the\n  encoded-source late-result interlock, redirects, and the divider's\n  ready-bus retirement gaps should stay at champion values. Preserve\n  effective-address low bits and store data across data backpressure.\n  Suppress requests and destination writes for misaligned accesses and\n  preserve aligned RVFI addresses, byte masks, traps, and in-order\n  channel-0 retirement. Check forwarded address bases from ALU and WB,\n  both displacement signs across a 4 KiB boundary, and a held store\n  following a producer.\n\n  With the same marker cycles, beating the 4.6% acceptance margin takes\n  more than 64.71 MHz median Gowin Fmax. Estimate 67 MHz and roughly 8%\n  higher fitness if the EX address path clears that threshold. Compare\n  all placement options and the remaining EX-to-fetch and divider paths,\n  and use the canonical stalled CoreMark run to check cycle count and CRCs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add a full effective byte address to EX/MEM separately from the integer writeback and bypass result.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Generate and register the load/store effective address directly from forwarded rs1 and the signed I/S offset, with the upper correction computed in parallel with the low sum. Hold it with the existing EX/MEM payload.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Use the dedicated address for bus outputs, lane masks, alignment, load extraction, and the aligned RVFI address. Keep the integer, load, and M writeback selection and hold behavior.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Exercise forwarded load and store bases, signed offsets crossing address boundaries, and address, write data, and retirement stability under data backpressure.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
