{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n e=json.loads(line)\n if e.get('category')=='structural' or e['id'].endswith('r4s0') or e['id'].endswith('r5s0'):\n  print(e['id'], list(e), e.get('title'))\n  for k in ['branch','outcome','result','fpga','notes','implementation_notes','timing_summary','hypothesis']:\n   if k in e: print(k, str(e[k])[:1800])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 ['id', 'title', 'category', 'motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes', 'outcome', 'fitness', 'delta_pct', 'vs_baseline', 'fmax_mhz', 'ipc_coremark', 'cycles', 'iterations', 'lut4', 'ff', 'seeds', 'perturbations', 'lutram', 'bsram', 'dsp', 'critical_path', 'logic_levels', 'formal_passed', 'cosim_passed', 'error', 'implementation_notes', 'timestamp', 'slot', 'round_id', 'lesson'] Share a multicycle divider across RV32M divide and remainder\noutcome improvement\nimplementation_notes # Shared multicycle divider\n\n`DIV`, `DIVU`, `REM`, and `REMU` now share a blocking unit. It registers the unsigned operand magnitudes and `3D` on launch, then advances the 36-bit dividend and quotient shift register and the remainder through three radix-4 digits per cycle for six cycles. Each digit uses three parallel 35-bit trial subtractions. Sign correction uses the held final state. The divisor-zero result falls out of the datapath because zero makes every trial fit. Quotient sign correction is disabled for divisor zero, and remainder sign correction reconstructs the original dividend. The 32-bit result also preserves the defined signed overflow behavior. `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` latches the existing substitute result at launch and uses the same phase counter and acceptance handshake.\n\nEX captures both forwarded operands for RVFI at launch. Fetch and ID/EX hold while EX/MEM receives bubbles and older instructions drain. Data-memory backpressure takes priority over launch and acceptance. On acceptance, EX/MEM receives the completed instruction and the following instruction can enter EX and use the normal bypass. The top-level interface follows `core.yaml` with single-channel RVFI. A guarded declaration include in `alu.sv` also supports the requested wildcard lint order.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Both normal and ALTOPS Verilator lint passed.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed on the first run with **53 checks passed, 0 failed**, including channel-0 liveness, unique order, and all four divide and remainder instr\n…[22238 of 25310 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:99a39193a3244b28]…\n next-stage write eligibility. Also compare the\\nsources with the current WB destination when it is writing. Snapshot\\nthat WB value in a shared 32-bit register on the same ID/EX capture\\nedge. Register four one-hot bits for each source in ID/EX. In priority\\norder, select the eligible fast EX writer, eligible MEM writer,\\nsnapshotted WB collision, or raw register-file read. Use a single\\nmasked OR mux per operand in EX. An x0 source selects zero. Invalid\\nentries and trapping writers are ineligible, including misaligned\\njumps in EX and misaligned loads in MEM. Keep architectural reset\\nand the standalone register-file write-first mode. Use its raw read\\nmode in core so the captured WB source supplies write-first behavior.\\n\\nFeed a dedicated load/store address adder from the selected rs1 and\\nthe decoded I/S displacement. Sum the low 12 bits with carry out\\nand compute base[31:12] plus one and minus one in parallel.\\nIncrement the upper word for a nonnegative offset with carry and\\ndecrement it for a negat\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
