{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +619.88%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM on the shared EX path with registered radix-4 rounds raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 39.190 MHz, with four cascaded steps per round setting the new timing limit and CoreMark's divide-free marker window staying at 4,491,485 cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +729.13%): Reducing the shared divider from four cascaded radix-4 steps per cycle to three moved the critical path out of the divider and raised median Gowin Fmax from 39.190 to 45.134 MHz, with a nine-cycle retirement gap after a redirect or load-use bubble on ready buses still within the ten-cycle liveness bound.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +3.96%): A fetch-local 16-entry tagged BTB saved 5.73% of CoreMark cycles by avoiding redirect bubbles on correct predictions, with the EX ALU still on the critical path and a drop in median post-placement Fmax from 5.442 to 5.334 MHz limiting the throughput gain.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.37%): Using valid-only fetch and decode bubbles removes the fetch NOP mux and ID/EX payload clear from redirect feedback, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the EX-to-dmem path and raising median Fmax from 45.134 to 47.560 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (regression, +8.99%): With valid-only squashes, registering bypass tag matches in ID and qualifying them with producer write eligibility at the one-hot EX operand mux removes tag comparisons and binary select encoding from execute, yielding a 49.189 MHz Gowin median at 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles with the multiply-result path to dmem setting the timing limit.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (improvement, +16.24%): Fetch-time JAL prediction and 64-entry gshare cut CoreMark cycles by 6.52% by avoiding taken-transfer bubbles, while carrying IF's direct targets into EX reduced recovery to a direction check and a one-hot EX result mux raised median Gowin Fmax from 43.967 to 49.042 MHz.\n- 2026-1\n…[4507 of 7579 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5b3d16bb7d96b68d]…\nich kept throughput below the round winner.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r7s0 (regression, +2.29%): Preselecting the alternate direct successor in ID/EX leaves a branch-comparator path to the registered miss pulse that limited one Gowin placement, while EX-to-dmem timing capped the median at 87.297 MHz and kept CoreMark throughput below the promotion threshold at unchanged cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r7s1 (improvement, +7.63%): Decoding alongside fetch target generation and storing control and immediates in a four-entry flow-through FIFO lets buffered issue start at register reads and forwarding checks, preserving the 5.98% stalled CoreMark cycle savings at a median Gowin Fmax of 86.362 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r7s2 (regression, +4.93%): Compressing the four-entry gshare FIFO to 73 bits with adder-free target reconstruction preserved the 5.98% stalled CoreMark cycle savings, with divider and ALU timing limiting Gowin Fmax to 84.192 MHz median and 76.603 MHz in the slowest placement.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -i '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20261122-002-r7s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-530\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Share a multicycle divider across RV32M divide and remainder\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench baseline runs at 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path spans\\nforwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path.\\nThe signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are the likeliest\\nsource of that depth. Their combinational results share the execute result\\nmux with ordinary ALU operations and memory addresses. Moving division to\\na registered u\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Retiming bypass selection across the decode-to-execute boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The registered divider moved the timing limit to the main pipeline at\\n45.134 MHz. Two of the three Gowin placement options reported their\\nlongest path from the register file through EX branch resolution, the\\nfetch instruction mux, and the load-use interlock back into ID/EX. The\\npath through operand delivery and the multiply result mux to data memory\\nis nearly as long. EX \n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Move RV32M result completion to the memory stage\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scored 116.81 iterations/s at a 49.042 MHz Gowin\\nmedian and 4,198,541 marker cycles. Its worst reported path crosses\\nthe execute ALU into EX/MEM, and other placements end in dmem RAM.\\nRegistering bypass comparisons in the previous structural candidate\\nleft the multiply-result path to dmem as the timing limit. Moving\\nmultiply completion to MEM in a scratch prototype sh\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Register divider operands before arithmetic setup\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 129.62 CoreMark iterations/s at a 54.869 MHz Gowin\\nmedian and 4,233,123 marker cycles. Its worst reported setup path starts\\nat a MEM/WB destination tag, crosses operand forwarding, and ends at\\nthe divider's launch remainder register after 22 cells and 24.117 ns.\\nEarlier measurements found no DIV/REM in the CoreMark marker window,\\nso this launch path can limit the b\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Preselect all execute operands and isolate data address generation\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 146.14 CoreMark iterations/s at 61.864 MHz\\nover 4,233,123 marker cycles. Its worst Gowin path starts at the\\ninferred register-file block RAM and reaches data memory through\\nthe register-file write-first mux, EX bypass, the integer ALU and\\nthe jump-link mux. Prior decode-stage tag matching left the write-first\\ndata mux in series with EX bypass\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Split divider rounds by divisor magnitude\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 182.41 CoreMark iterations/s at a 77.218 MHz Gowin\\nmedian over 4,233,123 marker cycles. Its critical path in all three\\nplacements crosses two dependent full-width radix-4 divider steps.\\nEarlier traces found no DIV/REM inside the marker window, but every\\ninstruction pays for the divider's clock period. A scratch redesign\\nshortened the divider path and exposed a register-fil\n21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Buffer decoded instructions ahead of register read and issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 201.60 CoreMark iterations/s at a median\\n85.341 MHz and 4,233,123 marker cycles. Its combinational IF/ID path\\naccepts a fetch only when decode can issue it immediately. A load-use\\ninterlock or dmem stall wastes a ready imem response, and an imem stall\\ncan leave EX idle after the back end becomes ready. Earlier fetch\\nqueues reduced marker cycles, bu\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
