{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_81", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r8s1.yaml && git status --short --untracked-files=all'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-002-r8s1\ntitle: Register fetch candidates and apply recovery on read\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 216.99 iterations/s at a median 86.362 MHz over\n  3,979,945 CoreMark marker cycles. Its reported 16.665 ns Gowin path starts\n  at the synthetic imem instruction source and ends at bit 18 of the normal\n  fetch request register. The decoded FIFO already hides stalls. The request\n  update still routes the computed direct target through the aperture and\n  successor decision before the address register, and EX redirect directly\n  resets both FIFO pointers and kills ID/EX validity. Registering only the\n  fetch address candidates preserved all marker cycles in a scratch test\n  and reached 87.023 MHz median. Its worst paths included the divider and\n  near-critical EX-to-fetch and EX-to-ID redirect paths. A registered squash\n  can remove the latter paths to the FIFO and ID/EX state before routing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the combined fetch next-PC register with separate registered word\n  candidates for the direct target, sequential successor, replay of the current\n  request, and EX recovery. Capture their inputs in parallel each edge. Register\n  the steering bits alongside them and use them to drive the next cycle's\n  imem address and PC for target generation, prediction indexing, and FIFO\n  packets. Bus ready, FIFO acceptance, direction and aperture checks determine\n  the normal successor selection. A registered redirect selects the separately\n  captured recovery word. This places a register after target arithmetic\n  and ahead of the wide address selection.\n\n  Use that same redirect bit to make the decoded FIFO logically empty in the\n  recovery cycle, with its logical head at the current registered tail. Rebase\n  the physical read pointer from there on the following edge, including a pop\n  if the corrected ready response bypasses the empty queue. The tail keeps\n  its normal acceptance rule. Tentative wrong-path captures stay unreachable\n  after the rebase. Apply the recovery bit t\n…[1580 of 4652 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:000163f25e83d97f]…\nre the issue candidate independently of EX redirect and qualify\n      the visible ID/EX valid bit with the registered squash. Preserve raw\n      operand forwarding and the existing hold rules for surviving entries.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: >-\n      Wire the registered recovery qualifier from fetch to issue so every\n      downstream instruction-valid consumer sees the squash on the same\n      edge as the corrected fetch address.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: >-\n      Verify next-cycle request addresses for a predicted target and a normal\n      fall-through. Exercise replay across imem stalls and a full FIFO,\n      recovery when the first target response stalls, and last-word release.\n      Check recovery at FIFO wraparound, including a coincident wrong-path\n      enqueue or issue candidate, and verify that only the selected path\n      retires or performs side effects. Run the existing single-channel checks\n      and canonical stalled CoreMark to check the cycle count and CRCs.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
