{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import sys,json; rows=list(map(json.loads,sys.stdin)); print(rows[0].keys()); print(rows[0].get(\\\"hypothesis\\\", {})); print([(r.get(\\\"id\\\"), r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"title\\\")) for r in rows])'\"", "aggregated_output": "dict_keys(['id', 'title', 'category', 'motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes', 'outcome', 'fitness', 'delta_pct', 'vs_baseline', 'fmax_mhz', 'ipc_coremark', 'cycles', 'iterations', 'lut4', 'ff', 'seeds', 'perturbations', 'lutram', 'bsram', 'dsp', 'critical_path', 'logic_levels', 'formal_passed', 'cosim_passed', 'error', 'implementation_notes', 'timestamp', 'slot', 'round_id', 'lesson'])\n{'motivation': 'The bench baseline runs at 5.442 MHz. Its 199.837 ns worst path spans\\nforwarding and the execute ALU before reaching the data-memory path.\\nThe signed and unsigned division and remainder operators are the likeliest\\nsource of that depth. Their combinational results share the execute result\\nmux with ordinary ALU operations and memory addresses. Moving division to\\na registered unit should shorten the clock period for every instruction.\\nThe baseline Verilator trace with both bus stall flags retired two DIVU\\ninstructions in the full CoreMark run and zero DIV/REM instructions in\\nthe 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles, so the timed loop has no direct\\ndivide latency cost.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Use one blocking, registered divider for DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU. On\\nissue, capture forwarded operands and form unsigned magnitudes. Register\\nthe divisor magnitude D and the 34-bit multiple 3D, using a shift for 2D.\\nProcess the 32-bit dividend magnitude with four leading zeros in six\\ncycles of three radix-4 restoring steps. For each step, append two input\\nbits to the current remainder, subtract D, 2D, and 3D in parallel with\\n35-bit borrow detection, and select the largest multiple that fits, or\\nzero. That choice yields the next two quotient bits and the new\\nremainder. Register the running remainder and quotient between cycles.\\nApply the quotient or remainder sign from the registered final state.\\nReturn the original dividend for remainder by zero and all ones for\\ndivision by zero. Preserve signed INT_MIN / -1 and its zero remainder.\\nKeep the same launch, wait, and acceptance schedule for edge cases an\n…[2395 of 5467 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:253ce6011a131ff7]…\nve the data-memory stall priority and existing load-use and redirect behavior.'}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Move the divide and remainder cases to the divider suite and keep base and multiply coverage on the ALU.'}, {'file': 'test/test_divider.py', 'description': 'Run all existing divide and remainder vectors against the clocked unit. Check reset, consecutive requests, latency, and result stability until acceptance, with signed overflow and divide-by-zero coverage intact.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Add the divider to the RTL source list. Place a divide between an older stalled memory operation and a dependent consumer, then check forwarded operands, memory side effects, and RVFI retirement and order.'}]}\n[('hyp-20261122-002-r1s1', None), ('hyp-20261122-002-r2s1', None), ('hyp-20261122-002-r3s1', None), ('hyp-20261122-002-r4s1', None), ('hyp-20261122-002-r5s1', None), ('hyp-20261122-002-r6s1', None), ('hyp-20261122-002-r7s1', None), ('hyp-20261122-002-r8s1', None)]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
