4 words, including both endpoints. The interval governs fills. Resident entries keep their exact tags when a different interval is trained.\n\nThe two parity banks register their current and successor matches from the selected registered request. A hold or sequential advance can use a staged match during an imem stall. Taken predictions and recoveries discard the preceding matches and start a lookup at the new request. Replay stores raw instructions, a narrow target, and branch/JAL classification bits. It uses the current gshare lookup and enqueues at the tail of the four-entry logical FIFO. Ready fetch retains its empty-queue bypass. Eight physical slots permit a tentative tail write each cycle.\n\nLive and replay targets have independent registered request candidates. The live packet carries the low target, alignment bit, carry, and displacement sign. EX reconstructs the wrapped upper target for recovery and RVFI. The last-word comparator uses a displacement-based range test to keep the low target adder off the aperture-hold path. FENCE.I invalidates the banks and staged fills at acceptance. A pending-fence count gates replay, loop training, and fills, while the ordinary gshare predictor continues to train from resolved branches. Recovery clears squashed fences and accounts for a fence accepted at the corrected PC.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all 117 cocotb cases passed. Co-simulation used `NRET=1` to match the core manifest and covered four ELFs both ready and stalled. The CoreMark CRCs passed with 3,549,156 stalled marker cycles, down 8.24% from r10s1.\n- Local single-issue formal reached 52 of 53 passes within the agent budget. `reg_ch0` was still running at the final check. An intermediate run hit a Yosys parser error when `$bits` was applied to the replay entry type. Taking `$bits` of the packed entry signal fixed elaboration. The formal wrapper holds imem ready high. Cocotb and stalled ISS comparisons exercise replay.\n- `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` confirmed Gowin placements of 82.844, 87.041, and 87.041 MHz with distributed RAM for the replay banks and FIFO. The 87.041 MHz median gives 245.24 iterations per second, down 1.98% from r10s1 and below the promotion gate. Paths from registered fetch selects into the FIFO write data limited all three placements. The option 0 build used 5,757 logic cells, 1,552 registers, and 131 LUT-RAM units.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
