{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -in '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|return.address|return stack|jalr|gshare|target buffer' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-610\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"16-entry tagged branch target buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core fetches sequentially and discards one fetch slot whenever\\nEX redirects a taken branch or jump. There is no IF/ID register, so a\\ncorrect fetch-stage prediction can remove that entire bubble. The baseline\\ntiming report puts the longest path through the ALU. The predictor can\\nimprove cycles per iteration while keeping its lookup local to fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core fetches sequentially and discards one fetch slot whenever\\nEX redirects a t\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Fetch-time direct targets with a 64-entry gshare predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench champion loses a fetch slot on each taken control\\ntransfer. The previous 16-entry tagged BTB saved 5.73% of CoreMark cycles,\\nbut its Fmax loss left a 3.96% fitness gain. In the champion's canonical\\nCoreMark trace, conditional branches and JAL account for 96% of taken\\ntransfers. Their targets are available from the fetched instruction. A\\nsmall direction table can capture most of the benefit while reducing EX\\nrecovery for direct transfers \n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Run the branch predictor ahead of decode with a two-entry fetch queue\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"In the champion's canonical CoreMark window, EX redirected 63,126 times.\\nThe 4.6% fitness hurdle at the champion's clock calls for saving about\\n185,000 cycles. Today the predictor also stops whenever decode stops.\\nKeeping prediction and fetch active across load-use, divide and dmem\\nholds lets the core use ready imem cycles that would otherwise go idle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"In the champion's canonical CoreMark window, EX redirected 6\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Run gshare ahead with a four-entry LUT-RAM fetch queue\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"EX redirects accounted for only about 63,000 cycles in the earlier gshare\n…[9759 of 12831 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:dedd3f9f88e9f9fc]…\nerations in 3,868,036 marker\\ncycles and scores 250.19 iter/s at a 96.774 MHz Gowin median. Its option-0\\nsetup path runs from the forwarded ALU result to mem_addr[26]. Rounds\\n12s0 and 12s1 targeted divider setup and the shifter, respectively, and\\nboth found the forwarding-to-address path limiting two placements. The\\nsplit address adder from r5s1 computes upper-word prefixes in parallel.\\nAddress generatio\n40:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Replay predicted short loops in place in the issue FIFO\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The r11s2 and r12s2 loop predictors eliminated 318,880 stalled CoreMark\\ncycles, but copying replay packets into the issue FIFO or adding a\\nsecond payload bank cost about 10 MHz. A retirement trace on r10s1\\nshows over 1.1 million dynamic instructions in uninterrupted taken\\nloop traversals of at most 16 words. A short loop can issue from FIFO\\nslots it already occupies. The 16-word physical depth fits the native\\nGowin distributed RAM depth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
