{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r14s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261122-003-r14s2\ntitle: Queue references to branch-trained instruction streams\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion takes 3,868,036 stalled CoreMark marker cycles.\n  Earlier loop replay reached 3,549,156 cycles, but copying replay\n  packets through the issue FIFO cost enough Gowin Fmax to erase the\n  gain. A predictor that retains several hot branch paths in block RAM\n  can cover stalls across loop boundaries. Queueing a slot number lets\n  each replayed instruction use its existing payload.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the four-entry flow-through issue FIFO and add a branch-trained\n  resident instruction store. Hash 64-byte PC blocks into a 64-bit\n  admission bitmap. A registered observation of a valid, advancing\n  taken conditional branch marks its source block, target block, and\n  the next sequential block after the target. Give the older late MEM\n  observation priority. Qualify the source and aligned target within\n  the 1 MiB imem aperture. The bitmap controls allocation only, so\n  aliasing there can admit extra fills. Every replay lookup checks\n  the exact word tag.\n\n  Fill 512 direct-mapped resident slots from accepted ready responses\n  in trained blocks. Stage the decoded payload and word index at live\n  acceptance. Maintain synchronous block RAM for compact decoded\n  payloads and two parity-banked metadata memories for tags, transfer\n  kinds and direct targets. Store the PC and eligible direct target\n  as 18-bit aligned words. Carry the exact full-width target on the\n  live path. Exclude JALR, FENCE.I, the last aperture word and direct\n  transfers with misaligned or out-of-aperture targets from replay.\n  Apply the same staged write decision to a slot's tag and payload.\n  Keep any slot referenced by the logical issue queue pinned through\n  its pop edge. Also suppress a staged fill that could overwrite the\n  current replay candidate on a stalled cycle.\n\n  Read metadata for the current normal request and its sequential\n  successor in parallel. Register the lookup address information,\n  occupancy and collision mask alongside the synchronous reads. Use\n  a staged lookup only for the matching held or sequential request.\n  Discard it after a taken prediction, a recovery, or a possible\n  same-slot fill on the lookup edge. A stalled imem cycle with FIFO\n  space may publish a replay reference and current gshare index and\n  direction. Predict eligible JAL as taken. Keep the live empty bypass.\n  Use the queue's next-head reference to read resident block RAM on\n  the same edge that advances the head. Hold the reference and payload\n  while issue stalls. The live FIFO can use eight physical slots with\n  four logical entries, so a tentative live write always targets a\n  spare slot. Let a replay enqueue update only the narrow reference\n  fields. Select the ready live bypass, live head or registered\n  resident head with one-hot masks.\n\n  Give live responses priority on every ready cycle. Register replay\n  steering independently of the live sequential, retry and direct\n  prediction enables. Merge the registered controls at the request\n  selection and let the older MEM redirect override EX and replay.\n  On recovery discard younger references and retain the corrected\n  live bypass. Use the last-word hold for every live response at the\n  final aperture word and let EX supply a taken successor there.\n  Clear the admission bitmap, resident valid bits and staged hits\n  on reset and accepted FENCE.I. Preserve already queued payloads.\n  Block replay and fills until accepted fences advance or are\n  squashed, including fence accounting on the corrected request.\n\n  A scratch prototype with branch-trained admission passed the\n  canonical CoreMark CRCs at 3,407,072 stalled marker cycles, an\n  11.92% reduction. At that cycle count promotion needs a Gowin\n  median above 89.17 MHz. A 90.5 MHz median would yield 265.62\n  iterations/s, a projected 6.17% gain. An earlier 64-slot shared\n  asynchronous payload prototype reached 66.639 MHz, with recovery\n  feeding replay lookup and live PC selection feeding payload writes.\n  The synchronous reads, staged fills, tentative live write and\n  independent registered replay steering target those setup paths.\n  Check all Gowin placements, stalled ISS traces, the canonical\n  CoreMark CRCs and the existing single-retire formal checks,\n  including the ready-bus retirement gap after recovery into DIV/REM.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Add the branch-trained block admission bitmap, staged resident fills, synchronous parity lookahead and replay references. Read the resident payload at head advance and keep the live FIFO write and recovery paths independent of replay.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \\\"Route the existing EX and late MEM branch outcomes, source PCs and exact targets to fetch with MEM priority. Wire the advancing FENCE.I event and retain the single-channel RVFI interface.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Expose a valid advancing FENCE.I event for fetch invalidation bookkeeping.\\\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \\\"Exercise resident-index aliases, fills competing with pinned and newly published replay references, FIFO wrap, staged reads and fence invalidation. Check EX and late MEM recovery before a younger store and check ready-bus recovery into DIV/REM.\\\"\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nimport jsonschema\nimport yaml\n\npath = Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r14s2.yaml')\nhypothesis = yaml.safe_load(path.read_text())\njsonschema.Draft7Validator(json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text())).validate(hypothesis)\nassert hypothesis['id'] == path.stem and hypothesis['category'] == 'predictor'\nassert all((Path('cores/bench') / change['file']).is_file() for change in hypothesis['changes'])\ncycles = 3_407_072\nprint(f'{path}: schema and paths passed')\nprint(f'Threshold: {250.19 * 1.046 * cycles / 10e6:.5f} MHz')\nprint(f'At 90.5 MHz: {90.5e6 * 10 / cycles:.5f} iterations/s')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
