{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261122-003-r(1s2|2s2|8s2|11s2|12s2|13s2|14s2)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; rows=list(map(json.loads,sys.stdin)); print(list(rows[-1])); [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"id\\\"]), print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"].get(\\\"hypothesis\\\")), print(str(r.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\", \\\"\\\"))[:10500])) for r in rows]'\"", "aggregated_output": "['id', 'title', 'category', 'motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes', 'outcome', 'fitness', 'delta_pct', 'vs_baseline', 'fmax_mhz', 'ipc_coremark', 'cycles', 'iterations', 'lut4', 'ff', 'seeds', 'perturbations', 'lutram', 'bsram', 'dsp', 'critical_path', 'logic_levels', 'formal_passed', 'cosim_passed', 'error', 'implementation_notes', 'timestamp', 'slot', 'round_id', 'lesson']\n\n hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\nAdd a 16-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer indexed by fetch PC[5:2].\nUse PC[19:6] as the tag, target[19:2] as the stored word address, a valid\nbit and a two-bit saturating counter. On a word-aligned fetch within the\n1 MiB instruction aperture, predict the cached target on a valid tag hit\nwith the counter's high bit set. Default to PC+4. Train conditional\nbranches toward their resolved direction, initializing on replacement to\nweakly taken or weakly not taken according to the observed outcome. Train\nJAL and JALR as strongly taken and refresh the resolved target. Allocate\nonly legal controls with aligned, in-aperture source and target addresses.\nStore the candidate direct target even on a not-taken branch. Read the\ntarget array only at the fetch index, and read tags and counters at both\nthe fetch and training indices. Reset only the valid bits so the payload\ncan infer distributed RAM.\n\nUpdate the fetch PC from the predictor only when IF can enter ID/EX.\nThe registered fetch PC is then the successor selected for the instruction\nin EX and stays paired with it through a dmem hold. For every valid EX\ninstruction that advances, including non-control instructions, compare its\nfull resolved next PC with that fetch PC. On a mismatch, give EX recovery\npriority over an imem stall and squash the younger instruction before it\nenters ID/EX. Train only on advancing EX instructions. Use the same\nresolved next PC for recovery and RVFI, including the existing sequential\nnext PC on misalignment traps.\n\nA scratch prototype running canonical CoreMark with both standard bus\nstalls reduced marker-bracketed cycles from\n…[31835 of 34907 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:09dd77038b146098]…\n fills disabled\nthrough the scrub. Preserve already queued payloads.\nBlock replay and fills until accepted fences advance or are\nsquashed, including fence accounting on the corrected request.\n\nA scratch prototype with branch-trained admission passed the\ncanonical CoreMark CRCs at 3,407,072 stalled marker cycles, an\n11.92% reduction. At that cycle count promotion needs a Gowin\nmedian above 89.163 MHz. A 90.5 MHz median would yield 265.62\niterations/s, a projected 6.17% gain. The single-read prototype\nreached 73.283 MHz. Its median setup path went from the resident\nRAM through issue hazard logic back to the next resident RAM read\naddress. A separate run also exposed the indexed validity bitmap\nwrite as a timing limit. Speculative parallel reads and in-RAM\nvalidity target those paths. Measure their timing and cycle impact.\nCheck all Gowin placements, stalled ISS traces, the canonical\nCoreMark CRCs and the existing single-retire formal checks,\nincluding the ready-bus retirement gap after recovery into DIV/REM.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
