{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -i '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|hyp-20261122-003-r[1-3]s2' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"16-entry tagged branch target buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core fetches sequentially and discards one fetch slot whenever\\nEX redirects a taken branch or jump. There is no IF/ID register, so a\\ncorrect fetch-stage prediction can remove that entire bubble. The baseline\\ntiming report puts the longest path through the ALU. The predictor can\\nimprove cycles per iteration while keeping its lookup local to fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core fetches sequentially and discards one fetch slot whenever\\nEX redirects a taken branch or jump. There is no IF/ID register, so a\\ncorrect fetch-stage prediction can remove that entire bubble. The baseline\\ntiming report puts the longest path through the ALU. The predictor can\\nimprove cycles per iteration while keeping its lookup local to fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 16-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer indexed by fetch PC[5:2].\\nUse PC[19:6] as the tag, target[19:2] as the stored word address, a valid\\nbit and a two-bit saturating counter. On a word-aligned fetch within the\\n1 MiB instruction aperture, predict the cached target on a valid tag hit\\nwith the counter's high bit set. Default to PC+4. Train conditional\\nbranches toward their resolved direction, initializing on replacement to\\nweakly taken or weakly not taken according to the observed outcome. Train\\nJAL and JALR as strongly taken and refresh the resolved target. Allocate\\nonly legal controls with aligned, in-aperture source and target addresses.\\nStore the candidate direct target even on a not-taken branch. Read the\\ntarget array only at the fetch index, and read tags and counters at both\\nthe fetch and training indices. Reset only the valid bits so the payload\\ncan infer distributed RAM.\\n\\nUpdate the fetch PC from the predictor only when IF can enter ID/EX.\\nThe registered fetch PC is then the successor selected for the instruction\\nin EX and stays paired with it through a dmem hold. For every valid EX\\ninstructio\n…[24489 of 27561 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7170e5937be53085]…\n enabled. Stalled CoreMark passed the canonical CRC and completion checks.\\n- Gowin timing across placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 48.531, 49.349, and 49.349 MHz. `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1` reproduced the 49.349 MHz median and measured 3,984,611 bracketed CoreMark cycles. The score was 123.85 iterations/s, 6.03% above the champion's 116.81 iterations/s and above the 4.6% hurdle.\\n\\nThe limiting setup paths run from the register file through EX forwarding and the ALU to data RAM. The median has about 0.66 MHz of margin over the qualification frequency at the measured cycle count.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T03:50:15.511949Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r3s2 (regression, +6.03%): A two-entry flow-through fetch queue cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 5.10% by filling during decode holds and draining during imem stalls, while the EX forwarding and ALU path to data RAM limited median Fmax to 49.349 MHz and left throughput below the round's MEM-stage RV32M winner.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md schemas/hypothesis.schema.json; ls -la cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +619.88%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM on the shared EX path with registered radix-4 rounds raised median Fmax from 5.442 to 39.190 MHz, with four cascaded steps per round setting the new timing limit and CoreMark's divide-free marker window staying at 4,491,485 cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +729.13%): Reducing the shared divider from four cascaded radix-4 steps per cycle to three moved the critical path out of the divider and raised median Gowin Fmax from 39.190 to 45.134 MHz, with a nine-cycle retirement gap after a redirect or load-use bubble on ready buses still within the ten-cycle liveness bound.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +3.96%): A fetch-local 16-entry tagged BTB saved 5.73% of CoreMark cycles by avoiding redirect bubbles on correct predictions, with the EX ALU still on the critical path and a drop in median post-placement Fmax from 5.442 to 5.334 MHz limiting the throughput gain.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +5.37%): Using valid-only fetch and decode bubbles removes the fetch NOP mux and ID/EX payload clear from redirect feedback, shifting the Gowin timing limit to the EX-to-dmem path and raising median Fmax from 45.134 to 47.560 MHz at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (regression, +8.99%): With valid-only squashes, registering bypass tag matches in ID and qualifying them with producer write eligibility at the one-hot EX operand mux removes tag comparisons and binary select encoding from execute, yielding a 49.189 MHz Gowin median at 4,491,485 CoreMark cycles with the multiply-result path to dmem setting the timing limit.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (improvement, +16.