{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_57", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,170p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r5s2.yaml && git status --short --untracked-files=all\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-003-r5s2\ntitle: Register the predictor's alternate PC in parallel with branch resolution\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The four-entry gshare fetch queue in r4s2 saved 253,178 stalled CoreMark\n  cycles. Its longest Gowin setup path ran from the register file through EX\n  recovery to the fetch PC. At 57.942 MHz it fell just below the 146.14\n  iter/sec champion. At the queue's measured 3,979,945 cycles, clearing the\n  4.6% acceptance hurdle takes a median above 60.84 MHz.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retime misprediction recovery for the four-entry flow-through gshare\n  frontend. Use the r4s2 queue organization with a packed asynchronous-read,\n  synchronous-write LUT-RAM payload, registered occupancy for physical writes,\n  and an empty-queue bypass. Use three-bit head and tail pointers with wrap\n  bits. Physically write the raw packet into the tail whenever a slot is\n  free. Advance the tail only for a ready response that enters the queue.\n  Backpressure fetch for a full queue and resume acceptance on the cycle\n  after a pop frees space. Keep the champion's 64-entry direction table,\n  resolved two-bit history, direct JAL prediction, and saved-index training.\n\n  For the instruction in EX, compute the sole possible misprediction\n  destination using its saved prediction. A predicted-taken direct transfer\n  can miss to PC+4. A predicted-not-taken direct transfer can miss to its\n  saved direct target. JALR is fetched sequentially, so its candidate comes\n  from the forwarded base plus immediate. Register this one full word address\n  on the EX resolution edge, independently of the resolved branch condition\n  and with no write enable from the miss signal. At that edge, also register a\n  separate miss bit from EX advance and the difference between the predicted\n  and actual aligned direction. In IF, OR together mutually exclusive masks\n  for the normal request PC, registered alternate address, and aperture-edge\n  resume constant. Use the selected word address for both imem and gshare.\n  The final next-PC select, qu\n…[2406 of 5478 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6b65ab50cdcc2f40]…\nparately. Predict from the effective request PC and use a full four-entry LUT-RAM FIFO with recovery-cycle bypass, retry, and boundary handling.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire the EX alternate address and recovery signals. Mask the younger ID/EX valid bit consistently for EX and the hazard unit on the recovery cycle.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Drive frontend consumption independently of imem readiness and gate late-result checks on packet validity. Preserve load-use bubble insertion, divide holds, and dmem holds.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Clear only validity for reset and load-use bubbles. Capture the recovery-cycle bypass under the usual decode acceptance rule.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Verify that a dmem hold permits fetch runahead and defers recovery and training until EX advances. Check both ready and stalled recovery responses, wrong-path store and divide discard, the aperture edge, and the exact divider and retirement-order checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
