{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_51", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --porcelain && nl -ba cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r6s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "     1\tid: hyp-20261122-003-r6s2\n     2\ttitle: \"Run gshare ahead of decode with a four-entry queue and registered recovery\"\n     3\tcategory: predictor\n     4\tmotivation: |\n     5\t  The r5s1 champion spends 4,233,123 cycles in the stalled CoreMark window\n     6\t  and its Gowin timing limit is the divider recurrence. The r5s2 predictor\n     7\t  frontend cut the same workload to 3,979,945 cycles, but its 64.667 MHz\n     8\t  median was limited by the older register-file-to-EX-ALU-to-dmem path.\n     9\t  Run that predictor frontend on r5s1's registered operand selects and\n    10\t  dedicated load/store address path to capture the cycle savings at the\n    11\t  champion's clock rate.\n    12\thypothesis: |\n    13\t  Decouple the gshare request PC from decode with a four-entry flow-through\n    14\t  FIFO for the instruction, PC, direct target, applied direction and saved\n    15\t  lookup index. Use a packed-vector array with asynchronous head read and\n    16\t  synchronous tail write. Registered head and tail pointers with wrap bits\n    17\t  determine free space for physical writes. Move the logical tail only for\n    18\t  a ready response that enters the FIFO, and move the head only when decode\n    19\t  accepts its packet. Bypass directly to decode when empty and ready. Advance\n    20\t  the request PC on a logical enqueue or an accepted bypass. Fetch\n    21\t  may fill while decode holds for a load-use dependency, divide or dmem stall,\n    22\t  and decode may drain during an imem stall. A full FIFO resumes accepting\n    23\t  responses on the cycle after a pop. Keep direct JAL prediction and the\n    24\t  existing gshare table, resolved history and once-per-advance training at\n    25\t  the saved index.\n    26\t\n    27\t  At the EX boundary, register a direction-miss bit and independently\n    28\t  capture the one possible recovery word address from the saved prediction\n    29\t  and transfer type. For predicted-taken direct controls it is PC+4, for\n    30\t  predicted-not-taken direct controls it is the saved target, and for JALR\n    31\n…[2921 of 5993 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:38f282727fc55475]…\nand advancing miss pulse. Preserve the champion's operand muxes, dedicated memory address adder, RV32M timing and resolved RVFI metadata.\"\n    71\t  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    72\t    description: \"Wire decode consumption and registered recovery. Mask wrong-path ID/EX validity for EX and hazard logic, and flush the held valid bit if recovery meets a decode stall. Preserve the existing operand selection and memory pipeline interfaces.\"\n    73\t  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    74\t    description: \"Separate decode consumption from imem readiness and derive dependency bubbles from the selected packet's validity. Preserve divide and dmem hold priorities.\"\n    75\t  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    76\t    description: \"Update the held-store test to verify predictor runahead and deferred EX recovery. Exercise queue fill, drain and wrap, stalled recovery retry, wrong-path store and divide discard, and recovery at the fetch aperture edge. Retain retirement order, branch training and the exact 9- and 10-cycle divider gaps.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
