{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -in '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|return address|\\\\bRAS\\\\b|branch target|speculative history' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-1900\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"16-entry tagged branch target buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core fetches sequentially and discards one fetch slot whenever\\nEX redirects a taken branch or jump. There is no IF/ID register, so a\\ncorrect fetch-stage prediction can remove that entire bubble. The baseline\\ntiming report puts the longest path through the ALU. The predictor can\\nimprove cycles per iteration while keeping its lookup local to fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core fetches sequentially and discards one fetch slot whenever\\nEX redirects a taken branch or jump. There is no IF/ID register, so a\\ncorrect fetch-stage prediction can remove that entire bubble. The baseline\\ntiming report puts the longest path through the ALU. The predictor can\\nimprove cycles per iteration while keeping its lookup local to fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 16-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer indexed by fetch PC[5:2].\\nUse PC[19:6] as the tag, target[19:2] as the stored word address, a valid\\nbit and a two-bit saturating counter. On a word-aligned fetch within the\\n1 MiB instruction aperture, predict the cached target on a valid tag hit\\nwith the counter's high bit set. Default to PC+4. Train conditional\\nbranches toward their resolved direction, initializing on replacement to\\nweakly taken or weakly not taken according to the observed outcome. Train\\nJAL and JALR as strongly taken and refresh the resolved target. Allocate\\nonly legal controls with aligned, in-aperture source and target addresses.\\nStore the candidate direct target even on a not-taken branch. Read the\\ntarget array only at the fetch index, and read tags and counters at both\\nthe fetch and training indices. Reset only the valid bits so the payload\\ncan infer distributed RAM.\\n\\nUpdate the fetch PC from the predictor only when IF can enter ID/EX.\\nThe regist\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Fetch-time direct targets with a 64-entry gshare predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Th\n…[10235 of 13307 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:012003f178cb039e]…\ning across decode\\nholds and supplying decode during instruction-bus stalls. Its 76.709 MHz\\nmeasurement used the previous divider and register file. A queue with\\nnarrower address fields can recover those cycle savings with less LUT-RAM\\nand routing alongside the champion's split divider and banked register file.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Run the champion's 64-entry gshare and direct JAL predictor at a request\\nPC that can advance independently of decode. Put four flow-through entries\\nbetween prediction and decode. Each queued packet carries its instruction,\\nsource word PC, exact direct target, applied direction, and lookup index.\\nFor a source in the 1 MiB fetch aperture, store only PC[19:2]. Even a\\nsigned JAL displacement leaves the full direct target between -1 MiB and\\n2 MiB, exclusive of the upper bound. Store target[20:2] and target[31].\\nExpand the target as {{11{target[31]}}, target[20:2], bit1, 1'b0}, deriving\\nbit1 from the stored instruction (bit 21 for JAL, bit 8 otherwise).\\nStore the two low bits \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
